'''Abigail Anne Spanberger<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://historical.elections.virginia.gov/candidate/100068 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260117064858/https://historical.elections.virginia.gov/candidates/view/Abigail-Anne-Davis-Spanberger/ |archive-date=2026-01-17 |publisher=Virginia Department of Elections |website=Historical Elections Database |title=Abigail Anne D(avis) Spanberger}}</ref>''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|s|p|æ|n|b|ɜr|g|ər}} {{respell|SPAN|bur|gər}}; {{Nee|'''Davis'''}}; born August 7, 1979) is an American politician and former intelligence officer serving since 2026 as the 75th [[governor of Virginia]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she served from 2019 to 2025 as the [[United States House of Representatives|U.S. representative]] for {{ushr|VA|7}}. 

Spanberger was born in New Jersey and moved frequently during her childhood before her family settled in Virginia. She earned degrees from the [[University of Virginia]] and [[Purdue University]]. From 2006 to 2014, she was an officer in the [[Central Intelligence Agency]]. She was elected to the U.S. House of Representatives in 2018, unseating incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Dave Brat]]. She was reelected in 2020 and 2022 before leaving Congress to run for governor.  

Spanberger was elected governor of Virginia in [[2025 Virginia gubernatorial election|2025]], defeating the Republican nominee, [[Lieutenant Governor of Virginia|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Winsome Earle-Sears]]. She is Virginia's first [[List of female governors in the United States|female governor]]. In her first months as governor, Spanberger signed an [[assault weapons]] ban, increased the [[List of U.S. states by minimum wage|state's minimum wage]], vetoed a [[Cannabis in Virginia|recreational cannabis]] retail market and [[collective bargaining]] legislation, and supported the [[2026 Virginia redistricting referendum]].

==Early life and education==
Spanberger was born Abigail Anne Davis<ref>{{Cite web |title=أبيغيل سبانبرغر.. ضابطة استخبارات تحكم ولاية فرجينيا |url=https://www.aljazeera.net/encyclopedia/2025/11/17/%d8%a3%d8%a8%d9%8a%d8%ba%d9%8a%d9%84-%d8%b3%d8%a8%d8%a7%d9%86%d8%a8%d8%b1%d8%ba%d8%b1-%d8%b6%d8%a7%d8%a8%d8%b7%d8%a9-%d8%a7%d8%b3%d8%aa%d8%ae%d8%a8%d8%a7%d8%b1%d8%a7%d8%aa-%d8%aa%d8%ad%d9%83%d9%85 |access-date=2026-05-04 |website=الجزيرة نت |language=ar}}</ref> in [[Red Bank, New Jersey]], on August 7, 1979,<ref>{{cite web |first=Nathan L. |last=Gonzales |title=Candidate Conversation - Abigail Spanberger (D) |url=https://www.insideelections.com/news/article/candidate-conversation-abigail-spanberger-d |work=[[Inside Elections]] |date=October 12, 2017 |access-date=November 7, 2018}}</ref> her father, Martin Davis, a police officer, and her mother, Eileen Davis, a nurse.<ref name=":4">{{Cite news |last=Tumulty |first=Karen |date=5 November 2025 |title=Spanberger's unlikely journey from the CIA to Va.'s first female governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/11/05/abigail-spanberger-virginia-governor/ |access-date=5 November 2025 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref><ref>Vozzella, Laura (November 12, 2023). [https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/other/virginia-rep-abigail-spanberger-to-run-for-governor-in-2025/ar-AA1jQhnN "Virginia Rep. Abigail Spanberger to Run for Governor in 2025."] ''[[MSN.com]]''. Retrieved November 13, 2023.</ref><ref>{{Cite web |author=PATRICK WILSON |date=November 9, 2018 |title=Spanberger on her victory: 'I want to be accountable' |url=https://richmond.com/spanberger-on-her-victory-i-want-to-be-accountable/article_6e9b963f-7bd7-5857-aaaf-1c7955cc20c9.html |website=Richmond Times-Dispatch}}</ref> She knew from a young age that she wanted to be a spy, writing her diary in [[Encryption|code]].<ref name=":4" />

Her family moved often when she was young, living in [[Maine]], the [[New York City area]], and [[Philadelphia]], before settling in [[Short Pump, Virginia]], when she was 13.<ref name=":4" /><ref name="RTD1" /> Her father had moved from policing to federal law enforcement for the [[United States Postal Inspection Service]]. She graduated from [[John Randolph Tucker High School]] and was later a [[legislative page|page]] for U.S. senator [[Chuck Robb]].<ref name="RTD1">{{cite web|title=Abigail Spanberger's passion for languages led her to the CIA|date=May 12, 2018 |url=https://www.richmond.com/news/virginia/government-politics/abgail-spanberger-s-passion-for-languages-led-her-to-the/article_d99787c8-8f5a-5e99-946c-be207565f62f.html|publisher=The Richmond Times-Dispatch |access-date=July 17, 2018}}</ref>

Spanberger earned a [[Bachelor of Arts]] from the [[University of Virginia]] in 2001<ref name=":4"/> and a [[Master of Business Administration]] from a joint program between the [[GISMA Business School]] in [[Germany]] and [[Purdue University]]'s [[Krannert School of Management]]. She initially enrolled at the [[College of William and Mary]] before transferring to the University of Virginia.<ref name="RTD1" /> At the time she had completed her undergraduate degree at the University of Virginia, Spanberger was conversationally fluent in Spanish and "five or six more" languages.<ref name=":4" />

==Career==
In the early 2000s, Spanberger taught [[English literature]] as a substitute teacher at the [[Islamic Saudi Academy]] in Northern Virginia.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Vozzella |first=Laura |date=September 11, 2018 |title=Saudi School Dominates TV Ads in Race for Suburban Richmond Congressional Seat |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/saudi-school-dominates-tv-ads-in-race-for-suburban-richmond-congressional-seat/2018/09/11/1f8ad70a-b5d4-11e8-a2c5-3187f427e253_story.html |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=September 17, 2019}}</ref> She received a conditional job offer from the [[Central Intelligence Agency]] (CIA) in December 2002.<ref name=":4"/> While waiting for a background check to be completed, Spanberger worked as a [[United States Postal Inspection Service|postal inspector]], as her father did, focusing on [[money laundering]] and narcotics cases.<ref name=":4" /><ref name="Examiner1">{{cite web|title=This former CIA officer says she can beat Virginia Rep. Dave Brat|url=https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/news/campaigns/abigail-spanberger-the-former-cia-officer-challenging-rep-dave-brat|publisher=The Washington Examiner website|access-date=July 17, 2018|date=May 30, 2018}}</ref>

In July 2006, after Spanberger's background check had been completed, she joined the CIA as a case officer, working to find, recruit, and build relationships with [[foreign national]]s who could have had information of value to the U.S. government.<ref name=":4" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Former CIA Officer Abigail Spanberger is on a mission for Virginia's 7th District|date=May 3, 2018 |url=https://rvamag.com/politics/virginia-politics/former-cia-agent-abigail-spanberger-is-on-a-mission-for-virginias-7th-district.html|publisher= [[RVA Magazine]]|access-date=July 17, 2018}}</ref> She has publicly said that she gathered intelligence about [[nuclear proliferation]] and [[terrorism]].<ref>{{cite web|title=The operative: Abigail Spanberger|date=June 6, 2018 |url=https://www.chesterfieldobserver.com/articles/the-operative-abigail-spanberger/|publisher=The Chesterfield Observer website|access-date=July 17, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181009204400/https://www.chesterfieldobserver.com/articles/the-operative-abigail-spanberger|archive-date=October 9, 2018}}</ref> Her first assignment was to [[Brussels]], according to ''The Washington Post''.<ref name=":4" /> During her intelligence career, she held five different [[passport]]s, and met people [[Cover (intelligence gathering)|undercover]].<ref name=":4" />

In 2014, Spanberger left the CIA and entered the private sector. She was hired to do consulting work for colleges and universities.<ref name=":4" /><ref>[https://www.wtkr.com/2019/01/04/who-are-they-virginias-fresh-faces-in-congress/ Who are they? Virginia's fresh faces in Congress], ''[[WTKR]]'', Nick Boykin, January 4, 2019. Retrieved November 14, 2020.</ref> After the [[2016 United States presidential election|2016 presidential election]], she began working with [[Emerge America]] to encourage women to run for state and congressional offices.<ref name=":4" /> In 2017, [[Virginia Governor|Virginia governor]] [[Terry McAuliffe]] appointed her to the Virginia Fair Housing Board.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Hall |first1=Madison |title=RESULTS: Nick Freitas squares off against Democratic incumbent Abigail Spanberger in Virginia's 7th Congressional District |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/nick-freitas-squares-off-against-incumbent-abigail-spanberger-in-virginias-7th-congressional-district/ar-BB18txqd |access-date=June 17, 2021 |work=www.msn.com |date=November 5, 2020}}</ref>

==U.S. House of Representatives==

=== Elections ===

==== 2018 ====
{{See also|2018 United States House of Representatives elections in Virginia#District 7}}
[[File:Abigail Spanberger Henrico Campaign Rally.jpg|thumb|Spanberger speaking at a campaign rally on election day eve in 2018]]
In July 2017, Spanberger announced her candidacy for the [[United States House of Representatives]] in {{ushr|VA|7}} in the [[2018 United States House of Representatives elections in Virginia|2018 election]] against incumbent [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] [[Dave Brat]], a former [[Tea Party movement]] member.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.msnbc.com/the-last-word/eric-cantor-loses-gop-primary-dave-brat|title=Eric Cantor loses GOP primary to tea party challenger Dave Brat|last=Kim|first=Clare|date=June 10, 2014|publisher=[[MSNBC]]| access-date=November 14, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.elle.com/culture/news/a46527/abigail-spanberger-to-run-against-republican-congressman-dave-brat/ |title=Abigail Spanberger becomes the fifth female candidate vying for the Democratic nomination to run against Republican Congressman Dave Brat |publisher=Elle.com |date=July 11, 2017 |access-date=November 4, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/its-grilling-time-five-women-line-up-to-challenge-rep-brat/2017/07/24/c71071ae-6bfe-11e7-9c15-177740635e83_story.html |title='It's grilling time': Five women line up to challenge Rep. Brat |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=July 24, 2017 |access-date=November 4, 2018}}</ref> She had begun to consider challenging Brat after attending a [[town hall]] meeting he hosted in [[Nottoway County]] in February 2017 and made the final decision to run in May after the House voted to repeal the [[Affordable Care Act]], texting her husband, "I'm gonna run and I'm gonna f---ing win".<ref name=":4" /><ref name="Examiner1" /> On June 12, 2018, Spanberger defeated Dan Ward in the Democratic [[Partisan primary|primary election]] with 73% of the vote, receiving more votes than any other candidate in the Virginia primaries that day.<ref>{{cite web |first=Patrick |last=Wilson |url=https://www.richmond.com/news/virginia/government-politics/abigail-spanberger-easily-defeats-dan-ward-in-th-district-democratic/article_8fb56fc7-89da-54b6-92b0-6274ed458f3f.html | title=Abigail Spanberger easily defeats Dan Ward in 7th District Democratic primary |publisher=The Richmond Times-Dispatch |date=June 12, 2018 |access-date=November 7, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Spanberger wins Democratic primary in Virginia's 7th Congressional District|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2018/live-updates/midterms/live-primary-election-results/spanberger-wins-democratic-primary-in-virginias-7th-congressional-district/|newspaper=The Washington Post|access-date=July 17, 2018}}</ref>

[[File:Abigail Spanberger, official 116th Congress photo portrait (cropped).jpg|thumb|Spanberger during the [[116th United States Congress|116th Congress]]]]In August, the [[Congressional Leadership Fund]], a super PAC closely aligned with Republican House Speaker [[Paul Ryan]], conducted a [[smear campaign]] against Spanberger. The campaign, which attempted to tie her to terrorism, was based on an [[Standard Form 86|SF-86]] application she completed to obtain security clearance, which was released in breach of privacy rules.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Harwood|first=John|date=August 31, 2018|title=Democratic House candidate Abigail Spanberger suffers the kind of election year smear John McCain would recognize|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/08/31/democrat-suffers-the-kind-of-smear-john-mccain-would-recognize.html|access-date=November 29, 2020|website=CNBC|language=en}}</ref> In a visit to the district, former Trump adviser [[Steve Bannon]] called the race "an absolute bellwether of the entire country" and said that losing it would mean Republican loss of control of the House.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Spinelli |first=Dan |title=Abigail Spanberger Just Beat Tea Party Darling Dave Brat in Virginia |url=https://www.motherjones.com/politics/2018/11/abigail-spanberger-just-beat-tea-party-darling-dave-brat-in-virginia/ |access-date=November 29, 2020 |website=Mother Jones |language=en-US}}</ref>

Spanberger won the November 6 general election by about 6,800 votes.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/elections/results/virginia-house-district-7|title=Virginia's 7th House District Election Results: Dave Brat vs. Abigail Spanberger|last=Almukhtar|first=Sarah|date=November 7, 2018|work=The New York Times|access-date=November 7, 2018}}</ref> Brat won eight of the district's ten counties, but Spanberger dominated the two largest counties, [[Henrico County, Virginia|Henrico]] and [[Chesterfield County, Virginia|Chesterfield]], by a combined margin of over 30,000 votes.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnn.com/election/2018/results/virginia/house |title=Virginia House |website=[[CNN]] |access-date=October 24, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181109153137/https://www.cnn.com/election/2018/results/virginia/house |archive-date=November 9, 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> Her campaign outraised Brat's, with $5.8 million ({{Inflation|index=US|value=5894065|start_year=2018|r=0|fmt=eq}}) to his $2.1 million ({{Inflation|index=US|value=2124110|start_year=2018|r=0|fmt=eq}}).<ref>{{cite web |title=Open Secrets breakdown of the 7th District |url=https://www.opensecrets.org/races/geography?cycle=2018&id=VA07 |access-date=July 17, 2018 |publisher=Open Secrets website}}</ref> After winning the election, Virginia Supreme Court Justice [[William C. Mims|Mims]] wrote her a letter extolling the virtues of public service, leadership, and civility, alongside a copy of former [[Czech President]] [[Václav Havel]]'s essay "Politics, Morality, and Civility". Spanberger has said the letter and its impact on her leadership were crucial to her development as a politician.<ref name=":6">{{Cite news |last=Schneider |first=Gregory |date=11 January 2026 |title=How a chance meeting led to an unlikely friendship for Virginia's next governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2026/01/11/virginia-governor-spanberger-judge-mims-havel/ |access-date=13 January 2026 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

Spanberger was the first Democrat to win this seat since 1970, when four-term Democrat [[John Otho Marsh Jr.|John Marsh]] retired and was succeeded by Republican [[J. Kenneth Robinson]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/11/01/democrat-abigail-spanberger-could-upset-gop-rep-dave-brat-in-virginia.html|title=After nearly 50 years of Republican control, this Virginia House district could flip to the Democrats|author1=Christina Wilkie|publisher=[[CNBC]]|date=November 11, 2018}}</ref> But until 1993, the 7th stretched from the outer Washington suburbs through the [[Shenandoah Valley]] and [[Charlottesville, Virginia|Charlottesville]] to the outer Richmond suburbs;<ref>See, for instance, [https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=34664 a map of the 7th in 1990]</ref> the present 7th is geographically and demographically the successor to what was the 3rd district before 1993.<ref>See, for instance, [https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=34659 a map of the 3rd in 1990]</ref> That district had been in Republican hands since 1981; former House majority leader [[Eric Cantor]] represented it from 2001 until Brat ousted him in the 2014 Republican primary.

Spanberger and her colleagues [[Elissa Slotkin]] and [[Mikie Sherrill]] were described as the "mod squad", a moderate alternative to the progressive "[[Squad (U.S. Congress)|squad]]".<ref name="i676">{{cite web |last=Goba |first=Kadia |date=2024-05-13 |title=Democrats are about to lose their mod squad |url=https://www.semafor.com/article/05/12/2024/democrats-are-about-to-lose-their-mod-squad |access-date=2025-06-19 |website=Semafor}}</ref><ref name="e004">{{cite web |last=Arrieta-Kenna |first=Ruairi |date=2019-01-18 |title='We Call Ourselves the Badasses': Meet the New Women of Congress |url=https://www.politico.com/interactives/2019/photos-new-women-of-congress/ |access-date=2025-06-19 |website=POLITICO}}</ref> Spanberger and Sherrill shared a [[Capitol Hill]] apartment for four years while they served in Congress together.<ref name=":4" />

==== 2020 ====
{{See also|2020 United States House of Representatives elections in Virginia#District 7}}

Spanberger faced a close reelection contest against [[Virginia House of Delegates|Virginia delegate]] [[Nick Freitas]], who represented much of the congressional district's northern portion. She won with 51% of the vote to Freitas's 49%. Freitas carried eight of the district's ten counties, as Brat had done two years earlier, but Spanberger prevailed by winning the district's shares of Henrico and Chesterfield counties by a combined 43,400 votes, five times her overall margin of 8,400 votes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cnn.com/election/2020/results/state/virginia/house/district-7 | title = Virginia House Results| publisher = CNN | access-date = November 13, 2023 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|title=Virginia Election Results: Seventh Congressional District|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|date=November 3, 2020 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/11/03/us/elections/results-virginia-house-district-7.html|access-date=November 6, 2020|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> She was also boosted by [[Joe Biden]] narrowly carrying the district;<ref>[https://results.elections.virginia.gov/vaelections/2020%20November%20General/Site/Presidential.html Virginia presidential results by congressional district] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210102102719/https://results.elections.virginia.gov/vaelections/2020%20November%20General/Site/Presidential.html |date=January 2, 2021 }} from Virginia Department of Elections</ref> Biden was the first Democrat to win what is now the 7th Congressional District since 1948.

On November 5, days after winning reelection by a margin of 1.8%,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Abigail Spanberger declares victory over Freitas in 7th Congressional District race|url=https://www.nbc12.com/2020/11/04/abigail-spanberger-declares-victory-over-freitas-th-congressional-district-race/|access-date=November 7, 2020|website=nbc12.com|language=en-US}}</ref> Spanberger criticized the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]]'s strategy for the 2020 elections in a phone call with other Democratic caucus members that was subsequently leaked.<ref>{{Cite news|date=November 5, 2020|title=Spanberger criticizes Democrats' strategy in caucus call|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/video/politics/spanberger-criticizes-democrats-strategy-in-caucus-call/2020/11/05/6ec2b368-258a-4061-9738-d83ee8971c3c_video.html|access-date=November 7, 2020|newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref> Calling the elections "a failure" from a [[United States Congress|congressional]] standpoint, she singled out Republican [[attack ad]]s decrying "[[Socialism in the United States|socialism]]" and the movement to "[[defund the police]]" as prime reasons the Democratic Party lost seats in [[Marginal seat|swing districts]]. Spanberger argued that Democrats should watch Republican ads before deciding how to talk about issues and never "use the word 'socialist' or 'socialism' ever again".<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|last=Siegel|first=Benjamin|date=November 6, 2020|title='It was a failure': House Democrats grapple over surprise 2020 losses|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/failure-house-democrats-grapple-surprise-2020-losses/story?id=74048497|access-date=November 27, 2020|website=ABC News|language=en}}</ref>

After the 2020 elections, Spanberger criticized Democratic messaging, arguing that progressive slogans such as "defund the police" and "socialism" had hurt candidates in swing districts. CNN political editor [[Chris Cillizza]] called her comments "hard truth" for the Democratic Party, adding that for Democrats to succeed in the 2022 and 2024 elections, they should "listen to the likes of Spanberger" rather than push for "the boldest possible progressive legislation".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Cillizza |first=Chris |date=November 6, 2020 |title=This Democratic congresswoman just spoke some hard truth to her party |url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/11/06/politics/abigail-spanberger-house-democrats-2020-election/index.html |work=CNN}}</ref>

==== 2022 ====
{{See also|2022 United States House of Representatives elections in Virginia#District 7}}

For her first two terms, Spanberger represented a district that stretched from the Richmond suburbs to the fringes of the Shenandoah Valley. After the [[2020 United States redistricting cycle]], Spanberger's district was radically redrawn, and no longer included her home in Henrico County. She considered not running for reelection in the new district before deciding to do so.<ref name=":4" /> Spanberger was seen as one of the most vulnerable incumbents of the 2022 election cycle, with pre-election polls projecting a close race with Republican [[Prince William County]] [[County Supervisor|supervisor]] Yesli Vega, a law enforcement officer endorsed by Governor [[Glenn Youngkin]] and former president [[Donald Trump]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-biden-virginia-stafford-congress-2d6c0e9415cdea83d02f6fc07a45943c | title=Trump endorses Yesli Vega, Spanberger's GOP challenger | website=[[Associated Press]] | date=October 28, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://starexponent.com/news/local/a-breath-fresh-air-youngkin-endorses-vega-for-virginias-7th-congressional-district/article_a038f9d5-54dc-54d9-9c6d-b3412b4f03f4.html | title='A breath fresh air'—Youngkin endorses Vega for Virginia's 7th Congressional District | date=July 16, 2022 }}</ref> Spanberger defeated Vega, 52% to 48%, the largest margin at the time in any election Spanberger had run in.<ref>{{cite news |title=Virginia Seventh Congressional District Election Results |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2022/11/08/us/elections/results-virginia-us-house-district-7.html |access-date=December 10, 2022 |work=The New York Times |date=November 8, 2022}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Spanberger narrowly defeats GOP challenger in closely watched House race in Virginia |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2022-election/spanberger-narrowly-defeats-gop-challenger-closely-watched-house-race-rcna55913 |access-date=November 10, 2022 |website=NBC News |date=November 9, 2022 |language=en}}</ref>

=== Tenure ===
==== Trump administration====
According to ''[[FiveThirtyEight]]''{{'}}s congressional vote tracker, Spanberger voted with President Trump 8.7% of the time.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bycoffe |first=Aaron |date=2017-01-30 |title=Tracking Congress In The Age Of Trump |url=https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/congress-trump-score/abigail-spanberger/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250306201940/https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/congress-trump-score/abigail-spanberger/ |archive-date=2025-03-06 |access-date=2025-06-14 |work=FiveThirtyEight |language=en-US}}</ref> In the [[2016 United States presidential election|2016 presidential election]], Trump won 50% of the vote to [[Hillary Clinton]]'s 44% in Spanberger's future [[Virginia's 7th congressional district|congressional district]].<ref>{{cite web | title=Introducing the 2017 Cook Political Report Partisan Voter Index | publisher=The Cook Political Report |url=https://www.cookpolitical.com/introducing-2017-cook-political-report-partisan-voter-index |first1=David |last1=Wasserman |first2=Ally |last2=Flinn |date=April 7, 2017 | access-date=April 13, 2020}}</ref>

On September 23, 2019, Spanberger joined six other freshman House Democrats with national security backgrounds in calling for an impeachment inquiry into Trump. They co-wrote a ''Washington Post'' opinion piece explaining their support for an impeachment inquiry, writing: "Congress must determine whether the president was indeed willing to use his power and withhold security assistance funds to persuade a foreign country to assist him in an upcoming election." They wrote that, if the allegations were true, they amounted to a "flagrant disregard for the law" and a "threat to all we have sworn to protect".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cisneros |first1=Gil |last2=Cow |first2=Jason |last3=Houlahan |first3=Chrissy |last4=Luria |first4=Elaine |last5=Sherrill |first5=Mikie |last6=Slotkin |first6=Elissa |last7=Spanberger |first7=Abigail |title=Seven freshman Democrats: These allegations are a threat to all we have sworn to protect |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2019/09/24/seven-freshman-democrats-these-allegations-are-threat-all-we-have-sworn-protect/ |access-date=July 28, 2020 |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=September 23, 2019}}</ref> Spanberger later announced that she would vote in favor of impeachment, saying, "The President's actions violate his oath of office, endanger our national security, and betray the public trust".<ref>{{cite web|title=Vulnerable Democrats to vote to impeach Trump|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2019/12/16/ben-mcadams-vote-to-impeach-trump-086215|last=Ferris|first=Sarah|date=December 16, 2019|publisher=Politico|access-date=December 16, 2019}}</ref>

On June 1, 2020, Spanberger tweeted criticism of Trump's reaction to the [[George Floyd protests]], a series of protests against [[police brutality]] that began in [[Minneapolis]] on May 26. On June 2, ''The Washington Post'' and ''[[The New York Times]]'' quoted Spanberger and several other high-profile former CIA analysts' interpretations of Trump's reaction to the protests as reminiscent of the reaction of totalitarian dictators on the brink of losing control of their dictatorships. "As a former CIA officer, I know this playbook, and I know the president's actions are betraying the very foundation of the rule of law he purports to support, the U.S. Constitution", she said.<ref name=nytimes2020-06-02/><ref name=Wapo2020-06-02/><ref name=ibtimes2020-06-03/> Spanberger criticized Trump after police used tear gas and rubber bullets on peaceful protestors and a priest during the George Floyd protests to clear a path so that he could have a [[Donald Trump photo op at St. John's Church|photo op in front of St. John's Episcopal Church]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Haltiwanger|first=John|title=Trump's tear gas photo-op was 'frightening' to authoritarianism experts, who warn that his behavior will only get worse without 'fierce opposition'|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/trump-teargas-photo-op-was-frightening-to-authoritarianism-experts-2020-6|access-date=June 15, 2020|website=Business Insider}}</ref><ref name=nytimes2020-06-02>{{Cite news|last=Edmondson|first=Catie|date=June 2, 2020|title=Trump's Response to Protests Draws Bipartisan Rebuke in Congress|language=en-US|work= [[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/02/us/politics/trump-congress-protesters-tear-gas.html|access-date=June 15, 2020|issn=0362-4331 | quote = Representative Abigail Spanberger, Democrat of Virginia, a former C.I.A. officer, called his response the type of action {{'}}undertaken by authoritarian regimes throughout the world.{{'}} | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200927072557/https://www.nytimes.com/2020/06/02/us/politics/trump-congress-protesters-tear-gas.html |archive-date=September 27, 2020 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Spanberger opposed Democrats' attempts to amend the [[Insurrection Act of 1807]], saying that amending the rarely used law would not accomplish what Democrats intended.<ref name=":2" />

==== Biden administration ====
According to ''[[PolitiFact]]'', Spanberger publicly disagreed with some of Biden's immigration policies that have not been subject to congressional votes, but she voted for all 73 bills and resolutions in the House of Representatives that Biden voiced support for.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.politifact.com/factchecks/2022/aug/11/yesli-vega/spanberger-agrees-biden-most-time/ |title=Spanberger agrees with Biden most of the time |work=[[PolitiFact]] |publisher=[[Poynter Institute]] |last1=Fiske |first1=Warren |date=August 11, 2022 |access-date=February 25, 2025}}</ref> In a November 2021 interview with the ''New York Times'', Spanberger criticized Biden after the [[2021 Virginia gubernatorial election]], saying, "Nobody elected him to be [[Franklin D. Roosevelt|F.D.R.]]; they elected him to be normal and stop the chaos."<ref name="Biden comments">{{cite news |last1=Cillizza |first1=Chris |title=This Democrat thinks Joe Biden fundamentally misunderstood his mandate |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/11/04/politics/abigail-spanberger-joe-biden-2021/index.html |access-date=November 4, 2021 |publisher=CNN |date=November 4, 2021}}</ref> She also said the Democrats had not sufficiently recognized that inflation was problematic.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.businessinsider.in/politics/world/news/moderate-democratic-rep-abigail-spanberger-says-americans-didnt-elect-biden-to-be-fdr/articleshow/87561059.cms|title=Moderate Democratic Rep. Abigail Spanberger says Americans didn't elect Biden to be FDR|access-date=November 14, 2021|date=November 7, 2021|author=John L. Dorman|work=[[Business Insider]]|archive-date=November 14, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211114193532/https://www.businessinsider.in/politics/world/news/moderate-democratic-rep-abigail-spanberger-says-americans-didnt-elect-biden-to-be-fdr/articleshow/87561059.cms|url-status=dead}}</ref> In December 2023, she was one of six House Democrats to send Biden a letter criticizing Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu's Gaza military strategy.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-12-18 |title=House Democrats send letter to Biden criticizing Netanyahu's military strategy - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/israel-hamas-war-gaza-house-democrats-send-letter-to-biden-criticizing-netanyahu/ |access-date=2026-06-09 |website=www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

