== 2025 gubernatorial election ==
{{main|2025 Virginia gubernatorial election}}

[[File:Spanberger for Governor logo.png|thumb|Spanberger's gubernatorial campaign logo]]
[[File:2025 Virginia gubernatorial election results map by county.svg|300px|thumb|right|Map of the results of the 2025 Virginia gubernatorial election]]
In 2020, during a meeting with then-[[Governor of Virginia]] [[Ralph Northam]], Northam suggested she should run for governor someday.<ref name=":4" /> Spanberger took his advice, and in November 2023 announced that she would not seek reelection to Congress and would instead run for governor of Virginia in the 2025 election.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Abigail Spanberger launches bid for Virginia governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/11/13/abigail-spanberger-virginia-governor-00126815|author-first1=Kierra|author-last1=Frazier|access-date=2025-06-21 |website=POLITICO |date=November 13, 2023 }}</ref> She secured the uncontested nomination in April 2025.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Collette |first1=Christopher |title=Spanberger confirmed to be Democratic nominee for Virginia governor |url=https://www.13newsnow.com/article/news/local/virginia/democrat-abigail-spanberger-virginia-governor-race-nomination/291-15928e93-3546-4e91-ba91-20cf331c6809 |work=[[WVEC]] |access-date=April 3, 2025 |language=en |date=April 3, 2025}}</ref>

In May 2025, while campaigning, Spanberger said she would not sign a bill to fully repeal Virginia's [[right-to-work law]] if elected governor.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Englander |first1=Tyler |date=May 8, 2025 |title=Abigail Spanberger says she won't sign bill to fully repeal Virginia's right-to-work law |url=https://www.wric.com/news/virginia-news/8news-exclusive-abigail-spanberger-says-she-wont-sign-bill-to-fully-repeal-virginias-right-to-work-law/ |access-date=27 June 2025 |publisher=ABC 8 News}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pope |first=Michael |date=2025-04-28 |title=Virginia's Right to Work law will again be a big campaign topic this year |url=https://www.wvtf.org/news/2025-04-28/virginias-right-to-work-law-will-again-be-a-big-campaign-topic-this-year |access-date=2025-09-03 |website=WVTF |language=en}}</ref>

Spanberger is one of the few Virginia gubernatorial candidates to refuse money from [[Dominion Energy]], instead getting donations from the [[Dominion Energy|anti-Dominion]] watchdog [[Clean Virginia]] alongside her running mates, who defeated Dominion-backed candidates.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-06-18 |title=Clean Virginia tops Dominion in Democratic primaries |url=https://www.virginiascope.com/clean-virginia-tops-dominion-in-democratic-primaries/|author-first1=Brandon|author-last1=Jarvis|access-date=2025-09-03 |website=Virginia Scope |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-04-17 |title=Clean Virginia and Dominion Energy face off again in statewide primary |url=https://www.virginiascope.com/clean-virginia-and-dominion-energy-face-off-again-in-statewide-primary/ |access-date=2025-09-03 |website=Virginia Scope |language=en-US|author-first1=Brandon|author-last1=Jarvis}}</ref> Her priorities on immigration include scrapping Youngkin's immigration order allowing local police to help carry out [[Deportation in the second Trump administration|Trump's ICE raids and deportation policy]]. She supports rejoining the [[Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative]], which Youngkin, a Republican, left after Northam had joined it.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Heckt |first=Shannon |date=2025-09-02 |title=Va. governor candidates weigh in on Chesapeake Bay Foundation conservation goals • Virginia Mercury |url=https://virginiamercury.com/2025/09/02/va-governor-candidates-weigh-in-on-resilience-plans-as-chesapeake-bay-foundation-pinpoints-conservation-goals/ |access-date=2025-09-03 |website=Virginia Mercury |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Abigail Spanberger elected Virginia governor in a historic first that boosts Democrats ahead of 2026 | website=[[Associated Press News]] | date=November 4, 2025 | url=https://apnews.com/article/virginia-first-female-governor-earlesears-spanberger-01f9854a94fdab6e5719096664ee9be1|author-first1=Olivia|author-last1=Diaz|author-first2=Bill|author-last2=Barrow}}</ref>

Spanberger was elected in a [[Landslide victory|landslide]], securing 58% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-11-11 |title=2025 November General |url=https://enr.elections.virginia.gov/results/public/virginia/elections/2025-November-General|access-date=2025-11-11 |website=Virginia Department of Elections |language=en-US}}</ref> The Republican nominee, incumbent lieutenant governor [[Winsome Earle-Sears]], received 42%. It was the largest margin of victory for a Democratic gubernatorial candidate in Virginia since [[Albertis Harrison]] received just under 64% of the vote in [[1961 Virginia gubernatorial election|1961]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Yancey |first=Dwayne |date=2025-11-05 |title=13 reasons why Spanberger and the Democrats won so big. Trump is just one of them. |url=https://cardinalnews.org/2025/11/05/13-reasons-why-spanberger-and-the-democrats-won-so-big-trump-is-just-one-of-them/ |access-date=2025-11-05 |website=Cardinal News |language=en-US}}</ref> The official vote margin was over 527,000. While Democrats won the three statewide races in Virginia in 2025, Spanberger's 15-point margin of victory was the largest.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Orlando |first=Sal |date=2025-11-05 |title=Spanberger, Hashmi and Jones win election in clean blue sweep |url=https://commonwealthtimes.org/2025/11/05/spanberger-hashmi-and-jones-win-election-in-clean-blue-sweep/ |access-date=2025-11-05 |website=The Commonwealth Times |language=en-US}}</ref> Spanberger outperformed [[Kamala Harris]] in the [[2024 United States presidential election in Virginia|2024 presidential election in the state]] by nearly 10 percentage points, while winning 99% of Harris's voters.<ref name="CNN 2025">{{Cite web |title=Exit polls for Election Results 2025 |url=https://www.cnn.com/election/2025/exit-polls/virginia/general/governor/0 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251116220548/https://www.cnn.com/election/2025/exit-polls/virginia/general/governor/0 |archive-date=November 16, 2025 |access-date=November 6, 2025 |website=[[CNN]]}}</ref>

== Governor of Virginia (2026–present) ==

=== Transition ===
On November 5, Spanberger announced her [[transition team]], which included [[Chris Lu]], [[Daun Hester]], and [[Yohannes Abraham]]. Honorary co-chairs of the team included many [[Democratic Party of Virginia]] leaders, such as [[Louise Lucas]], [[Don Scott (Virginia politician)|Don Scott]], [[Jennifer Wexton]], and [[Rick Boucher]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Eggleston |first=Luke |date=5 November 2025 |title=Virginia Governor-Elect Spanberger identifies transition team |url=https://www.wusa9.com/article/news/local/virginia/virginia-governor-elect-spanberger-identifies-transition-team/65-89458980-c600-4e28-826d-4aaa284ff0e4 |access-date=7 November 2025 |website=wusa9.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Spanberger's transition informally began on November 6 with a lunch with Governor Youngkin.<ref name=":5">{{Cite news |last=Schneider |first=Gregory |date=6 November 2025 |title=Campaign jabs give way to 'lovely' lunch between Youngkin and Spanberger |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/11/06/spanberger-youngkin-virginia-governor-transition/ |access-date=7 November 2025 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> On November 12, Spanberger asked the University of Virginia's board of visitors to pause its search for a new president until her transition was complete, drawing criticism from both Youngkin and conservative alumni of the university for supposed politicization of the board process.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rosenzweig-Ziff |first=Dan |date=13 November 2025 |title=Youngkin rebukes Spanberger's ask for U-Va. to pause presidential search |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/11/12/spanberger-uva-president-search/ |access-date=14 November 2025 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> Spanberger said Youngkin had done the same during his tenure, and promised structural reform to prevent it.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Rosenzweig-Ziff |first1=Dan |last2=Schneider |first2=Gregory |date=22 November 2025 |title=Spanberger blasts Youngkin for politicizing Va. college boards, vows changes |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2025/11/22/spanberger-blasts-youngkin-virginia-college-boards/ |access-date=24 November 2025 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> Spanberger made her first cabinet appointment on December 2, announcing Marvin Figueroa as Secretary of Health and Human Resources,<ref>{{Cite web |author=MICHAEL MARTZ |date=2025-12-03 |title=Spanberger begins Cabinet rollout with health secretary pick |url=https://richmond.com/news/state-regional/government-politics/article_cd52608d-b79a-4265-a74d-767657aebb1b.html |access-date=2025-12-16 |website=Richmond Times-Dispatch |language=en}}</ref> and continued to make appointments throughout the month.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Schneider |first=Gregory |date=28 December 2025 |title=Here's who Virginia's next governor has chosen for her Cabinet so far |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/12/23/virginia-spanberger-cabinet-nominees/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260113155935/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2025/12/23/virginia-spanberger-cabinet-nominees/ |archive-date=13 January 2026 |access-date=13 January 2026 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

===Inauguration===
[[File:Governor Spanberger sweaing in (Beyer, 2026) (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|Spanberger being sworn in as Governor alongside her family]]
Spanberger was sworn in as governor on January 17, 2026.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Schneider |first1=Gregory S. |last2=Cox |first2=Erin |date=January 17, 2026 |title=Abigail Spanberger sworn in as Virginia's first female governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2026/01/17/spanberger-inauguration-virginia-governor/ |access-date=January 17, 2026 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref><ref name=firstfemalegovernor>{{cite news|url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/watch-live-abigail-spanberger-inaugurated-as-virginias-first-woman-governor|title=WATCH LIVE: Abigail Spanberger inaugurated as Virginia's first woman governor|publisher=PBS|date=January 17, 2026|access-date=January 17, 2026}}</ref><ref name=":5" /> She was sworn in by former [[Supreme Court of Virginia]] Justice [[William C. Mims]], with her husband Adam holding the Bible and her daughters by her side.<ref name=firstfemalegovernor /><ref name=":4" />

