==== First days and executive actions ====
On January 17, 2026, her first day in office, Spanberger signed ten executive orders focusing primarily on affordability, healthcare, housing, education, and preparedness for potential federal policy changes under the Trump administration.<ref name="governor.virginia.gov">{{Cite web |date=January 17, 2026 |title=Governor Spanberger Signs Day One Executive Orders to Make Virginia More Affordable |url=https://www.governor.virginia.gov/newsroom/news-releases/2026/january-releases/name-1081466-en.html |access-date=January 28, 2026 |website=Office of the Governor of Virginia}}</ref><ref name="vpm.org">{{Cite news |date=January 17, 2026 |title=Here are the 10 executive orders Gov. Abigail Spanberger signed on her first day |url=https://www.vpm.org/news/2026-01-17/governor-abigail-davis-spanberger-eos-trump-hr1-dei-287g-ice |access-date=January 28, 2026 |website=Virginia Public Media}}</ref> Key orders included a statewide affordability directive requiring agencies to identify cost-reduction measures; establishment of task forces on health financing and economic resiliency amid federal cuts; a review of housing regulations and creation of a housing production commission; directives strengthening public education and inclusive practices; a review of university board appointment processes; expanded authority for the chief of staff; a non-discrimination and equal opportunity policy with affirmative recruitment measures; and rescission of former Governor Glenn Youngkin's Executive Order 47 requiring state and local law enforcement cooperation with federal civil immigration enforcement.<ref name="governor.virginia.gov"/> Democrats praised the orders as delivering on campaign promises to lower costs and protect vulnerable populations, while Republicans criticized several as reversing effective Youngkin-era policies on immigration enforcement and merit-based education, and expressed concerns over the promotion of diversity initiatives and delegation of broad emergency powers.<ref name="vpm.org"/>

One of Spanberger's first challenges as governor was managing the [[January 23–27, 2026 North American winter storm|January 2026 North American winter storm]], responsible for the deaths of four people<ref>{{Cite web|last=Domingo|first=Ida|date=January 25, 2026|title=Man clearing snow among 3 dead in weather-related incidents in Fairfax County|url=https://wjla.com/news/local/fairfax-county-three-dead-weather-related-deaths-extreme-cold-dangerous-police-warning-cpr-calls-springfield-virginia-winter-safety-vulnerable-residents-icy-conditions-snow|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=WJLA}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last1=Bachmann|first1=C. Max|last2=Williams|first2=Molly|date=January 25, 2026|title=Winter storm delivers snowy, icy mix; 1 killed in Frederick County crash; schools closed|url=https://www.winchesterstar.com/winchester_star/winter-storm-delivers-snowy-icy-mix-1-killed-in-frederick-county-crash-schools-closed/article_3a89f154-6af9-5087-83fd-f0888e7fad97.html|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=[[The Winchester Star]]}}</ref> and hundreds of traffic accidents.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Zinn|first=Brad|date=January 28, 2026|title=Virginia State Police reported over 500 crashes during winter storm|url=https://www.newsleader.com/story/news/local/2026/01/28/virginia-state-police-release-final-crash-tally-following-winter-storm/88377107007/?gnt-cfr=1&gca-cat=p&gca-uir=false&gca-epti=z116732p119350c119350u113932e005900v116732&gca-ft=257&gca-ds=sophi|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=[[The News Leader]]}}</ref> On January 22, she issued a [[state of emergency]] ordinance, mobilizing the Virginia Department of Emergency Management to work with state agencies, local governments, and private-sector partners to ensure preparedness during the winter storm, and promulgated guidelines for Virginians so that they could remain safe.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 22, 2026|title=Governor Spanberger declares State of Emergency in advance of winter weather expected this weekend|url=https://www.vdot.virginia.gov/news-events/news/statewide/governor-spanberger-declares-state-of-emergency-in-advance-of-winter-weather-expected-this-weekend.php|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=[[Virginia Department of Transportation]]}}</ref>

On January 30, Spanberger described the Virginia Leaders in Export Trade (VALET) program, promoting [[free trade]] in opposition to Trump's tariffs. The [[Virginia Port Authority]] is a major hub of [[international trade]].<ref name="Trade Accelerator">{{Cite web|url=https://www.governor.virginia.gov/newsroom/news-releases/2026/january-releases/name-1111969-en.html|title=Virginia Companies Expand Into Global Markets Through Commonwealth's Trade Accelerator Program|first1=Abigail|last1=Spanberger|website=Governor of Virginia|access-date=January 30, 2026|date=January 30, 2026|quote=On Day One as Governor, I directed multiple state agencies to help increase export opportunities across the Commonwealth as Virginia businesses continue to feel the impacts of the federal administration's tariff policies. International trade has always been a key driver of Virginia's economic growth.}}</ref>

==== Address to the General Assembly ====
On January 19, 2026, Spanberger delivered her first address to a joint session of the [[Virginia General Assembly]], emphasizing affordability, bipartisanship, and pragmatic solutions. She committed to reentering the [[Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative]] (RGGI), maintaining Virginia's [[right-to-work law]], collaborating with the federal government where possible, and pushing back against policies harming Virginia jobs or families. She outlined legislative priorities including lowering healthcare and energy costs, protecting renters, expanding housing production, supporting agriculture amid tariffs, implementing pro-worker measures such as paid family leave and a higher minimum wage, and bipartisan gun safety bills.<ref name="cardinalnews.org">{{Cite news |date=January 19, 2026 |title=Spanberger pushes affordability and unity in her first General Assembly address |url=https://cardinalnews.org/2026/01/19/spanberger-pushes-affordability-and-unity-in-her-first-general-assembly-address |access-date=January 28, 2026 |website=Cardinal News}}</ref> Republicans welcomed the calls for unity and right-to-work commitment but proposed alternative tax relief measures and cautioned against increased spending.<ref name="cardinalnews.org"/>

==== Notable early legislation ====
In the opening days of the 2026 legislative session, the Democratic-controlled General Assembly advanced a proposed constitutional amendment to enshrine reproductive rights—including abortion, contraception, and fertility treatments—in the Virginia Constitution, passing it on party-line votes for consideration on the November 2026 ballot.<ref name="amendment-cite">{{Cite news |date=January 19, 2026 |title=Virginia lawmakers send reproductive rights amendment toward November vote |url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/01/19/virginia-lawmakers-send-reproductive-rights-amendment-toward-november-vote |access-date=January 28, 2026 |website=Virginia Mercury}}</ref> Spanberger, who campaigned in support of these amendments, said she would campaign for their passage.<ref name="amendment-cite" />

===="Affordable Virginia Agenda"====
On December 18, 2025, Spanberger announced her legislative priorities, the "Affordable Virginia Agenda", to reduce Virginians' cost of living by lowering the costs of prescription drugs, reining in pharmaceutical companies and [[price gouging]], increasing access to medical services in rural areas, improving healthcare price transparency, limiting [[prior authorization]]s for care, bolstering renter protections, boosting the supply of [[mixed-income housing]], streamlining the process by which the state finances [[affordable housing]] projects, lifting certain [[zoning]] and [[Construction permit|permitting]] restrictions, increasing the state's local energy generation, and expanding energy storage infrastructure to better satisfy the energy loads at times of peak demand, among other proposals to reduce healthcare, housing, and energy costs.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Abigail's Affordable Virginia Plan|url=https://abigailspanberger.com/issue/abigails-affordable-virginia-plan/|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=Abigail Spanberger for Virginia|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Making your bills more affordable|url=https://abigailspanberger.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Spanberger_EnergyAffordabilityPlan_L4.pdf|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Abigail Spanberger for Virginia|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Spanberger unveils plan to make Virginia more affordable|url=https://abigailspanberger.com/issue/abigails-affordable-virginia-plan/|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=Virginia Scope|date=December 18, 2025|last=Jarvis|first=Brandon|language=en-US}}</ref>

In the 2026 legislative session, the Democratic-controlled General Assembly worked to pass the entirety of this agenda, including renter protections, an energy efficiency task force,<ref name="ReferenceA">{{Cite web |date=March 14, 2026 |title=Governor Spanberger Statement on Sine Die Adjournment of Legislative Session, Passage of Full Affordable Virginia Agenda |url=https://www.governor.virginia.gov/newsroom/news-releases/2026/march-releases/name-1114561-en.html |access-date=16 March 2026 |website=Office of the Governor of Virginia}}</ref> carbon tax-funded energy bill relief,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Can a carbon price lower power bills? Virginia is betting yes.|url=https://grist.org/energy/spanberger-virginia-rggi-carbon-price-data-centers-dominion/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=[[Grist (magazine)|Grist]]|date=May 1, 2026|last=Bittle|first=Jake|language=en-US}}</ref> [[child care]] employer/state cost sharing program to reduce costs for employees,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Virginia passes new laws to help lower childcare costs, expand access|url=https://www.wric.com/news/politics/capitol-connection/virginia-passes-new-laws-to-help-lower-childcare-costs-expand-access/|access-date=May 2, 2026|website=WRIC|date=April 29, 2026|last=Dookan|first=Deniel|language=en-US}}</ref> regulations on the excess fees on premiums healthcare providers can charge for tobacco use,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Health insurance; tobacco surcharge.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB220|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 2, 2026}}</ref> and allowing [[manufactured homes]] to be installed in any residential zoning district that allows the construction of traditional homes, except historic districts.<ref>{{Cite web|title=New Virginia law will expand zoning for manufactured homes|url=https://www.vpm.org/generalassembly/2026-04-07/maldonado-vanvalkenburg-grumbine-manufactured-housing-virginia|access-date=May 2, 2026|website=VPM|date=April 7, 2026|last=Mirshahi|first=Dean|language=en-US}}</ref>

On March 30, 2026, the Assembly passed the Affordable Medicine Act, which makes the "[[Presidency of Joe Biden#Prescription medication|maximum fair prices]]" for select prescription drugs negotiated by the [[Centers for Medicare and Medicaid]] under the [[Inflation Reduction Act]] for [[Medicare (United States)|Medicare]] enrollees apply to the same drug purchases by Virginia-regulated health plans and state employee plans, while allowing [[Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974|ERISA]]-regulated plans the option to opt in. The Act created a Prescription Drug Affordability Advisory Panel charged with developing policy recommendations and strategies to improve prescription drug affordability, and [[pharmacy benefit manager]]s are required to report administrative fees, formulary management fees, rebate retention, and network access fees to the Panel.<ref name="mintz-cite">{{Cite web|title=States Continue to Advance New PBM and Drug Pricing Legislation in 2026|url=https://www.mintz.com/insights-center/viewpoints/2146/2026-04-09-states-continue-advance-new-pbm-and-drug-pricing|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=Mintz|date=April 9, 2026|last1=Carnegie|first1=Theresa C.|last2=Callander|first2=Grace|last3=Trautz|first3=Sarah|language=en-US}}</ref>

Effective July 1, 2026, HB1214 reduces the price cap for a 30-day supply of [[Insulin (medication)|insulin]] from $50 to $35, irrespective of the amount of insulin or its type, and sets an aggregate cost-sharing cap of $35 for a 30-day supply of diabetes equipment and supplies, including blood glucose meters and strips, continuous glucose monitors and supplies, and insulin pump supplies.<ref name="mintz-cite" /> SB669 prohibits pharmacy benefit managers from charging "unreasonable" fees for processing electronic claims, reversing claims without prior written notice or just cause, unfairly retaliating against pharmacies for exercising various rights guaranteed by law, and reducing payments to pharmacies to effective reimbursement rates not agreed to in a provider agreement. It builds upon a law preventing PBMs from engaging in "spread pricing", which increases the cost of prescription drugs, by ordering them to use a "pass-through" pricing model, and directs them to give 100% of rebates received from their associated drug manufacturers to the carrier, plan, or covered person to reduce deductibles, co-payments, coinsurance, or other cost-sharing amounts.<ref name="mintz-cite" /><ref>{{Cite web|title=Pharmacy benefits managers; requirements, application of law, report, delayed effective date.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/SB669|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 2, 2026}}</ref> HB736 limits prior authorizations for prescription medication that can lead to costly delays in care.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Health insurance; required provisions regarding prior authorization for prescription drugs.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB736|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 2, 2026}}</ref>

SB74 extends the state affordable housing program to all county-level jurisdictions and independent cities, and authorizes local authorities to ease single-family zoning and permit the construction of new [[Duplex (building)|duplexes, triplexes, and quadruplexes]], allow the construction of [[accessory dwelling unit]]s, allow the conversion of office, light industrial, and commercial space to multi-family housing, encourage [[transit-oriented development]], lower minimum lot sizes, and administer incentives/retract disincentives to spur affordable housing developments in areas where development would otherwise never happen, to increase housing density and supply to address the state's housing shortage and reduce prices.<ref>{{Cite web|title=New Virginia economic laws: Gov. Spanberger signs legislation addressing cost of living, health care|url=https://www.wtvr.com/news/local-news/spanberger-signs-new-virginia-laws-april-8-2026|access-date=May 13, 2026|website=WTVR|date=April 8, 2026|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Affordable housing; local zoning ordinance authority.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/SB74|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 2, 2026}}</ref> HB352 allows localities to incentivize affordable housing construction through a performance-based grant system,<ref>{{Cite web|title=Local authority; authorized by ordinance to establish affordable housing performance grant programs.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB352|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 2, 2026}}</ref> while HB594 allows local zoning administrators to expedite rezoning applications for housing developments consisting 75% of housing units affordable for households with incomes at or below the area median income, and allows the Department of Housing and Community Development to give localities priority for grants and loans issued therefrom for these projects.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Housing bills to boost supply, protect tenants on way to Spanberger's desk|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/03/17/housing-bills-to-boost-supply-protect-tenants-on-way-to-spanbergers-desk/|access-date=May 13, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]|date=March 17, 2026|last=Woods|first=Charlotte Rene|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Affordable housing developments, certain; expedited approval.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB594|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 2, 2026}}</ref>

On April 30, 2026, the Faith in Housing Act was signed into law. It eliminates the rezoning step for faith-based organizations and other tax-exempt nonprofits to develop affordable housing on their properties, allowing religious organizations to give their communities affordable housing with fewer regulatory restrictions.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Faith in Housing bill crosses finish line, clears path for church-based affordable housing|url=https://www.kvue.com/article/syndication/associatedpress/faith-in-housing-bill-crosses-finish-line-clears-path-for-church-based-affordable-housing/616-a1588a06-9919-4b00-acaa-1ecd726a2546|access-date=May 2, 2026|website=KVUE|date=April 30, 2026|last=Leayman|first=Emily|language=en-US}}</ref>

To aggregate the state's distributed energy generation and storage resources into a centralized system, increasing the grid's reliability, flexibility, and cost efficiency, HB1467 and HB562 expand Virginia's [[virtual power plant]] pilot program, which previously had directed only [[Dominion Energy]] to participate,<ref>{{Cite web|title=In Puerto Rico, customers are helping to keep the lights on. Could a Virginia program do the same?|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2025/09/02/in-puerto-rico-customers-are-helping-to-keep-the-lights-on-could-a-virginia-program-do-the-same/|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]|date=September 2, 2025|last=Main|first=Ivy|language=en-US|archive-date=September 11, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250911135635/https://virginiamercury.com/2025/09/02/in-puerto-rico-customers-are-helping-to-keep-the-lights-on-could-a-virginia-program-do-the-same/|url-status=live}}</ref> to include [[Appalachian Power]]<ref>{{Cite web|title=Bill to lower energy costs passes General Assembly|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/03/12/bill-lower-energy-costs-passes-general-assembly/|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=WDBJ7|date=March 12, 2026|last=Thomas|first=Pat|language=en-US|archive-date=March 12, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260312200610/https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/03/12/bill-lower-energy-costs-passes-general-assembly/|url-status=live}}</ref> and [[Utility cooperative#Electric cooperatives|electric utility cooperatives]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Electric cooperatives; authorized to establish and implement a virtual power plant program.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB562|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 1, 2026}}</ref> HB284/SB371 direct power utilities to establish a voluntary demand flexibility program for high-load energy users such as [[data center]]s, and incentivize reductions in energy use during times of high demand and offloading energy use to lower-demand periods to lower bills for all other ratepayers, alongside reducing the need to construct new, expensive power plants and attenuating grid stress at peak demand.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Electric utilities; electric demand flexibility programs, high energy demand customers, report.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB284|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 1, 2026|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=SUPPORT HB284 / SB371|url=https://vcnva.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/HB284-SB371-Talking-Points.pdf|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=Virginia Conservation Network|language=en-US}}</ref> SB377 allows utilities to cooperate with high-load energy users to build new substations that exclusively serve them, at the expense of the energy user, to shield other ratepayers from increased costs incurred by their intensive consumption.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Spanberger marks first 100 days with focus on healthcare, housing and energy affordability|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/28/spanberger-marks-first-100-days-with-focus-on-healthcare-housing-and-energy-affordability/|access-date=May 2, 2026|date=April 28, 2026|last=Woods|first=Charlotte Rene|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]|language=en-US}}</ref>

The Fair and Affordable Electric Rates and Reliability Act extended Dominion Energy and Appalachian Power's weatherization and bill assistance programs for low-income, elderly and disabled customers, and capped ratepayer costs to fund Dominion's electric line undergrounding program at 4% of the rate base.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Electric utilities; pilot program for energy assistance and weatherization for certain individuals.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB1393|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=Virginia General Assembly|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Virginia Lawmakers Pass 15 Data Center Bills as Tax Exemption Fight Looms|url=https://www.multistate.us/insider/2026/3/30/virginia-lawmakers-pass-15-data-center-bills-as-tax-exemption-fight-looms|access-date=May 7, 2026|date=March 30, 2026|last1=Scarboro|first1=Morgan|last2=Miller|first2=Kim|website=Multistate|language=en-US}}</ref> Spanberger returned the bill to the legislature with amendments on April 13, 2026. One amendment modified the language of a provision that directed the State Corporation Commission to discretionarily approve or deny measures to prevent certain high-load customers of 25 megawatts or greater from shifting the cost of new electricity distribution infrastructure onto other ratepayers with more stringent language to "take all measures" to reasonably ensure that other ratepayers are not subsidizing costs associated with this energy usage with rate hikes. Another amendment removed an exemption for high-load projects approved before July 1, 2026.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The governor's amendments to energy and data center legislation will save money for customers|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/22/the-governors-amendments-to-energy-and-data-center-legislation-will-save-money-for-customers/|access-date=May 7, 2026|date=April 22, 2026|last=Main|first=Ivy|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]|language=en-US}}</ref><ref name="legi-oks-cite">{{Cite web|title=Va. lawmakers OK governor's tweaks to major energy bills, reject health and labor bill amendments|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/23/va-lawmakers-ok-governors-tweaks-to-major-energy-bills-reject-health-and-labor-bill-amendments/|access-date=May 7, 2026|date=April 22, 2026|last1=Woods|first1=Charlotte Rene|last2=Heckt|first2=Shannon|last3=Willis|first3=Samantha|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]|language=en-US}}</ref> The General Assembly accepted these amendments while rejecting a number of other amendments to the bill.<ref name="legi-oks-cite" />

==== Energy and environment ====
Shortly after taking office in January 2026, Spanberger reiterated a pledge and long-term policy goal of the state Democratic Party to rejoin the Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative in her first address to the General Assembly.<ref>{{Cite web| last= Heckt| first= Shannon| date= January 22, 2026 | title= Spanberger, Democratic lawmakers are pressing for Virginia to rejoin RGGI. Here's how it would work.|url= https://virginiamercury.com/2026/01/22/spanberger-democratic-lawmakers-are-pressing-for-virginia-to-rejoin-rggi-heres-how-it-would-work/| access-date= May 9, 2026| website= [[Virginia Mercury]]| archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260406190953/https://virginiamercury.com/2026/01/22/spanberger-democratic-lawmakers-are-pressing-for-virginia-to-rejoin-rggi-heres-how-it-would-work/| archive-date= April 6, 2026| url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= Affordability Data Tool: Explore Virginia's Re-entry into the Regional Greenhouse Gas Initiative |url= https://www.rff.org/publications/data-tools/affordability-data-tool-explore-virginias-re-entry-into-the-regional-greenhouse-gas-initiative/ |access-date= 2026-08-12 |website= Resources for the Future |language= en-US}}</ref> A provision of a budget bill passed in late February directed state agencies to repeal the RGGI exit regulation and reinstate the previous framework governing its participation by June 2026, while HB397 amends state law to clarify a statutory requirement compelling the state to participate in the interstate [[cap-and-trade]] program.<ref>{{Cite web| last= Stankova| first= Theodora| date= May 7, 2026 |title= Virginia Enacts Legislation To Rejoin RGGI, Re-Entry Expected By Late May| url= https://carbonherald.com/virginia-enacts-legislation-to-rejoin-rggi-re-entry-expected-by-late-may/| access-date= May 9, 2026| website= Carbon Herald}}</ref>

In March 2026, Spanberger hosted the 2026 Governor's Environmental Excellence Awards, where she discussed the importance of responsible stewardship of the environment and announced award winners across eleven conservancy groups for their actions in preserving and protecting the state's natural resources and population from harmful development and disasters.<ref>{{Cite web|date=March 25, 2026|last=Olinger|first=Heather|title=Spanberger announces 2026 Governor's Environmental Excellence Award winners|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/03/25/spanberger-announces-2026-governors-environmental-excellence-award-winners/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=WDBJ7}}</ref> On April 8, she signed into law HB507, which prohibits the [[Virginia Department of Environmental Quality]] from issuing air permits to data centers with backup diesel gen-sets that do not satisfy the [[Environmental Protection Agency]]'s [[United States vehicle emission standards|tier 4 emission standards]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=February 17, 2026|last=Heckt|first=Shannon|title=Lawmakers debate how to regulate data centers' diesel backup generators|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/02/17/lawmakers-debate-how-to-regulate-data-centers-diesel-backup-generators/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Data centers; permit requirements, emission limits for certain engine-generator sets.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB507|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Virginia General Assembly}}</ref>

On April 22, to commemorate [[Earth Day]], Spanberger signed an executive order committing Virginia to the [[United States Climate Alliance]], a bipartisan coalition of governors committed to sustaining America's fight against climate change with state-led action.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 23, 2026|title=During Earth Week, Virginia Governor Abigail Spanberger Joins the U.S. Climate Alliance|url=https://usclimatealliance.org/press-releases/earth-week-virginia-joins-alliance/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=United States Climate Alliance}}</ref> In May, she signed a slate of legislation aimed at preserving the state's natural resources and treating drinking water for [[PFAS]] compounds.<ref>{{Cite web|date=May 6, 2026|last=Thomas|first=Pat|title=Governor Spanberger signs bills designed to protect natural resources|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/05/06/governor-spanberger-signs-bills-designed-protect-natural-resources/|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=WDBJ7}}</ref>

During her campaign, Spanberger committed to expanding the state's [[Solar power|solar]] and [[wind energy]] capacity.<ref name="ISC-cite">{{Cite web|date=August 3, 2025|title=Inside Climate News: Virginia's Democratic Gubernatorial Candidate Embraces Renewables, Natural Gas and Community Planning for Data Centers|url=https://abigailspanberger.com/inside-climate-news-virginias-democratic-gubernatorial-candidate-embraces-renewables-natural-gas-and-community-planning-for-data-centers/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Abigail Spanberger for Governor}}</ref> In office, she has worked to promote [[nuclear power]]<ref>{{Cite web|date=October 11, 2025|title=Both gubernatorial candidates are in support of nuclear energy|url=https://www.wvtf.org/news/2025-10-11/both-gubernatorial-candidates-are-in-support-of-nuclear-energy|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=WVTF}}</ref> and novel innovations such as next-generation nuclear reactors and [[nuclear fusion]],<ref>{{Cite web|last=Pampaloni|first=Hanna|date=April 1, 2026|title=5 Loudoun Bills Included in Spanberger's First Round of Bill Signing|url=https://www.loudounnow.com/news/5-loudoun-bills-included-in-spanberger-s-first-round-of-bill-signing/article_570e33de-fd4f-4321-8e81-1b63da61ec03.html|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Loudoun Now}}</ref> but she has acknowledged the need to continue to invest into [[natural gas]] power generation to preserve the cost of living for ratepayers and meet the state's short-term energy needs.<ref name="ISC-cite" />

In April 2026, Spanberger signed HB590, which establishes an Internet-based "Smart Solar Permitting Platform", set to launch on July 1, 2027, that automates plan review and automatically issues permits to the construction of [[residential solar]] installations that comply with the Uniform Statewide Building Code and other applicable state laws in order to streamline the regulatory approval process.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 13, 2026|last=Hartz|first=Sam|title=Smart solar permitting bill passes the finish line|url=https://solarbuildermag.com/policy/gov-spanberger-signs-suite-of-solar-storage-bills-for-virginia/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Solar Builder}}</ref> She also signed HB395, which prohibits landlords with more than four housing units and local governments preventing tenants from installing [[Balcony solar power|plug-in solar devices]], and prevents investor-owned or municipal utilities and electric cooperatives from imposing interconnection requirements, charging any fee related to the device, or requiring that customers obtain the utility's approval before installing or using the device, allowing apartment residents to generate their own solar power.<ref>{{Cite web|date=March 10, 2026|last=Heckt|first=Shannon|title=Plug-in solar panels near approval by General Assembly|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/03/10/plug-in-solar-panels-near-approval-by-general-assembly/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]}}</ref> The bipartisan Distributed Generation Expansion Act, also signed in April, increases Virginia's solar potential by expanding the distributed generation carveout of the Virginia Clean Economy Act from 1% to 4.5% from 2026 to 2030 compliance years, then 5% from 2031 to 2045 compliance years, boosting the development of residential and distributed solar.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 14, 2026|last=McConnell|first=Martin|title=Gov. Spanberger signs suite of solar, storage bills for Virginia|url=https://solarbuildermag.com/policy/gov-spanberger-signs-suite-of-solar-storage-bills-for-virginia/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Solar Builder}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=April 29, 2026|last=Purekal|first=Jim|title=Virginia's 2026 Legislative Session Delivered Major Clean Energy Wins|url=https://advancedenergyunited.org/blog/virginias-2026-legislative-session-delivered-major-clean-energy-wins/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Advanced Energy United}}</ref>

On March 25, Spanberger created the cabinet-level position of Chief Energy Officer via executive order, appointing [[Southern Environmental Law Center]] attorney Josephus Allmond to the position. The office is tasked with cooperating with the [[Virginia Secretary of Commerce and Trade]], the Virginia Department of Energy, [[PJM Interconnection]], and energy utility providers to address rising energy costs, expand clean energy generation, and develop a statewide energy strategy.<ref>{{Cite web|date=March 25, 2026|title=Governor Spanberger announces new cabinet-level Chief Energy officer|url=https://www.wsls.com/news/local/2026/03/25/governor-spanberger-announces-new-cabinet-level-chief-energy-officer/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=WSLS}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=March 25, 2026|last=Durrett|first=Tasha|title=Allmond joins Spanberger administration as first ever Chief Energy Officer in Virginia|url=https://www.selc.org/press-release/allmond-joins-spanberger-administration-as-first-ever-chief-energy-officer-in-virginia/|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=Southern Environmental Law Center}}</ref>

On April 9, Spanberger signed a bill instructing the Department of Energy to identify and develop training resources and curricula to expand the workforce allocated to projects relating to the [[offshore wind]] industry, prioritizing training for veterans, local workers, and people from historically economically disadvantaged communities.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 9, 2026|title=Effort to advance offshore wind workforce among bills signed by Spanberger|url=https://www.wtkr.com/news/politics/effort-to-advance-offshore-wind-workforce-among-bills-signed-by-spanberger|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=WTKR}}</ref> She later signed HB1444, formally codifying the Clean Energy Innovation Bank that had been trialed by the Virginia Department of Energy in 2024, which financed clean energy projects, greenhouse gas emissions reduction projects, and other similar projects.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Schmidt|first=Markus|date=May 15, 2026|title=Spanberger signs assault weapons ban, package of criminal justice and energy bills|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/05/15/spanberger-signs-assault-weapons-ban-package-of-criminal-justice-and-energy-bills/|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]}}</ref>

==== Immigration ====
On her first day in office, Spanberger signed the rescission of an executive order issued by Glenn Youngkin directing local and state law enforcement agencies to enter [[287(g)]] memoranda of agreement with [[United States Immigration and Customs Enforcement]], [[Deputy|deputizing]] federal immigration law enforcement authority to local officers. The rescission order does not force local departments to exit existing agreements with ICE.<ref>{{cite web|title=Spanberger's executive order does not immediately halt VSP cooperation with ICE|url=https://www.virginiascope.com/spanbergers-executive-order-does-not-immediately-halt-vsp-cooperation-with-ice/|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=January 18, 2026|website=Virginia Scope|last=Jones|first=Sean}}</ref> Nevertheless, the order has been criticized by Republicans and ICE officials, who have called it a "[[sanctuary state]]" policy that has led to an increase in immigrant crime.<ref>{{cite web|title=DHS urges Spanberger not to release illegal immigrant charged with child sex crime|url=https://wgxa.tv/news/nation-world/dhs-urges-virginia-governor-abigail-spanberger-not-to-release-illegal-immigrant-orge-enrique-garcia-rodriguez-charged-with-child-sex-crime-ice-enforcement-franklin-county|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=May 20, 2026|website=WGXA|last=Minock|first=Nick}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=DHS can't stop posting about Spanberger|url=https://www.politico.com/newsletters/national-security-daily/2026/04/10/dhs-cant-stop-posting-about-spanberger-00867486|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=May 20, 2026|website=[[POLITICO]]|last1=Bazail-Eimil|first1=Eric|last2=Ewing|first2=Giselle Ruhiyyih}}</ref>

On May 20, 2026, Spanberger signed into law HB1482, which makes it a class 1 [[misdemeanor]] for ICE officers to wear facial coverings or mask except to protect them from infection or ingesting toxic substances, and directs the Department of Criminal Justice Services to develop a model policy for and restrictions on the use of facial coverings by law-enforcement officers, while noting that only 30% of people held in ICE detention centers in Virginia have criminal backgrounds.<ref>{{cite web|title=Governor issues executive order to "protect Virginia residents from... aggressive federal immigration enforcement"|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/05/20/governor-issues-executive-order-protect-virginia-residents-aggressive-federal-immigration-enforcement/|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=May 20, 2026|website=WDBJ7|last=Thomas|first=Pat}}</ref> At the same time, she vetoed a law that would have prevented ICE from conducting civil arrests at courthouses, schools, and healthcare facilities.<ref>{{cite web|title=JUST IN: Spanberger signs one bill to rein in ICE, vetoes another|url=https://www.ffxnow.com/2026/05/20/just-in-spanberger-signs-one-bill-to-rein-in-ice-vetoes-another/|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=May 20, 2026|website=FFX Now|last=Woolsey|first=Angela}}</ref>

Spanberger simultaneously issued Executive Order 16, which directs state-level executive branch agencies to ascertain that federal immigration law enforcement officers entering, accessing, or using nonpublic areas possess a valid warrant authorizing their access to these properties; prevents federal immigration officers from using those properties as a staging area, processing location, or operations base for law enforcement; and provides guidance for courtroom, education, health and election officials in engaging with immigration officers in order to curtail attempts to intimidate citizens of Virginia.<ref>{{cite web|title=Spanberger signs order limiting ICE activity at polling places, schools and hospitals|url=https://wjla.com/news/local/virginia-immigration-spanberger-ice-enforcement-limiting-activity-schools-raids-arrests-executive-order-federal-agents-state-property-ban-courthouse-polling-locations|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=May 20, 2026|website=WJLA|last=Domingo|first=Ida}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=EXECUTIVE ORDER 16 (2026): A SAFER VIRGINIA FOR ALL|url=https://www.governor.virginia.gov/media/governorvirginiagov/governor-of-virginia/pdf/eo/eo-16-a-safer-virginia-for-all.pdf|access-date=May 20, 2026|date=May 20, 2026|website=Office of the Governor of Virginia}}</ref>

==== Reproductive rights ====
The Virginia House of Delegates voted to approve an [[2026 Virginia Right to Reproductive Freedom Amendment|amendment]] enshrining abortion access for all Virginia citizens shortly before Spanberger took office. The Senate followed suit shortly thereafter.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Sherman|first=Carter|date=January 16, 2026|title=Virginia voters to decide on amendment to protect abortion rights|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jan/16/virginia-vote-abortion-rights|access-date=May 10, 2026|website=[[The Guardian]]}}</ref> Spanberger signed the bill and sent it to the voters for final approval on February 6, 2026.<ref name="amend-cite">{{Cite news|last=Woods|first=Charlotte Rene|date=February 6, 2026|title=Spanberger signs bills to send constitutional amendments to voters this year|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/02/06/spanberger-signs-bills-to-send-constitutional-amendments-to-voters-this-year/|access-date=March 16, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]}}</ref> If passed by voters, the measure will amend the state constitution to provide that "every individual has the fundamental right to reproductive freedom and that such right shall not be, directly or indirectly, denied, burdened, or infringed upon."<ref>{{Cite news|title=Constitutional amendment (second reference); fundamental right to reproductive freedom.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HJ1|access-date=May 10, 2026|website=Virginia General Assembly}}</ref>

On April 8, Spanberger signed the Right to Contraception Act, enshrining Virginians' right to obtain and use [[Contraception|contraceptive]] drugs or devices and healthcare providers' right to provide them.<ref>{{cite web|title=Contraception; definitions, establishes right to obtain, applicability, enforcement.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB6|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 10, 2026}}</ref> On April 22, she signed the Contraception Equity Act, which requires that health insurers cover the cost of [[Food and Drug Administration]]-approved contraceptive drugs or devices on an outpatient basis, prohibits them from imposing copayments or other cost-sharing fees onto the insured except in certain circumstances, and requires that any insurance plan provide point-of-sale coverage without cost-sharing at in-network pharmacies for hormonal contraceptives available over the counter.<ref>{{cite web|title=Health insurance; coverage for contraceptive drugs & devices, including over-the-counter.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/SB361|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 10, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Woods|first=Charlotte Rene|date=April 1, 2026|title=Virginia lawmakers send contraception-access bills to governor's desk|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/01/virginia-lawmakers-send-contraception-access-bills-to-spanberger/|access-date=May 10, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]}}</ref>

==== Labor and employment ====
On April 6, 2026, Spanberger signed into law HB1, which would increase the state's hourly [[Minimum wage in the United States|minimum wage]] from $12.77 to $13.75 by January 1, 2027, and $15 by 2028; thereafter the minimum wage will be indexed to increases in the [[consumer price index]] and hiked incrementally every year.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Gov. Spanberger signs bill to increase Virginia's minimum wage to $15 an hour|url=https://www.nbcwashington.com/news/politics/gov-spanberger-signs-bill-to-increase-virginias-minimum-wage-to-15-an-hour/4089056/|access-date=May 12, 2026|website=NBC4 Washington|date=April 10, 2026|last=Wilder|first=Drew|language=en-US|archive-date=April 11, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260411084241/https://www.nbcwashington.com/news/politics/gov-spanberger-signs-bill-to-increase-virginias-minimum-wage-to-15-an-hour/4089056/|url-status=live}}</ref> Two days later, she signed HB20, which rescinded a statutory exemption from the state minimum wage law for farm laborers and employees, which would go into effect on January 1, 2027.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Minimum wage; farm laborers or farm employees, temporary foreign workers.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB20|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 12, 2026}}</ref>

Also on April 6, Spanberger signed SB149, which expands Virginia's state-run [[Individual retirement account|individual retirement savings program]], RetirePath, to an estimated 357,000 private-sector workers employed by businesses that do not offer qualified workplace retirement plans by lowering the full-time or part-time business employment minimum for enrollment in the program to five employees from 25. The law also drops the requirement that employees work at least 30 hours weekly.<ref>{{Cite web| title= Virginia retirement program triples in size |url= https://kenbridgevictoriadispatch.com/2026/07/15/virginia-retirement-program-triples-in-size/ |website= The Kenbridge Victoria Dispatch |date= July 15, 2026| access-date= July 21, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web| title= State-facilitated IRA savings program; various changes to the program. |url= https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/SB149 |website= Virginia General Assembly}}</ref> Contemporaneously, Spanberger signed a bill expanding [[workers compensation]] for [[first responder]]s experiencing [[post-traumatic stress disorder]].<ref>{{Cite web| title=Charlottesville delegate's bills on PTSD, sexual extortion signed into law |url= https://www.29news.com/2026/04/13/charlottesville-delegates-bills-ptsd-sexual-extortion-signed-into-law/ |access-date= May 12, 2026 |website= 29 News |date= April 13, 2026 |last= Womack| first= Gabby| language= en-US}}</ref>

In April 2026, Spanberger also signed laws heavily restricting [[non-compete agreement]]s, including SB170, which makes non-compete agreements unenforceable if an employee is fired "without cause" unless the employer provides severance benefits or another monetary payment, which must be disclosed upon the agreement's execution.<ref>{{Cite web| last1= Levy| first1= Daniel R.| last2= Dano| first2= Gianna| last3= Emanuelson, Jr. |first3= Eric I.| title= Virginia Approves SB170: Expanded Restrictions on Enforcement of Noncompetes Take Effect July 1, 2026 |url= https://natlawreview.com/article/virginia-approves-sb170-expanded-restrictions-enforcement-noncompetes-take-effect |website= [[The National Law Review]] |date= April 16, 2026 |access-date= May 13, 2026}}</ref> On April 22, she signed the Equal Pay Act, which prohibits employers from asking prospective workers about their pay history and using that data in considering whether to hire them or in determining their salary unless they voluntarily provide their pay history. She also signed a bill that expands legal protections against [[wage theft]].<ref>{{Cite web| last= Israel| first= Josh| title= Virginia legislators give final approval to paid leave, wage theft, and equal pay laws| url= https://virginiaindependentnews.com/politics/virginia-legislators-give-final-approval-to-paid-leave-wage-theft-and-equal-pay-laws/ |website= Virginia Independent News |date= April 23, 2026 |access-date= May 16, 2026}}</ref>

On May 11, Spanberger signed a bill establishing a Paid Family and Medical Leave Insurance program administered by the [[Virginia Employment Commission]], set to go into effect in 2028.<ref>{{Cite web|last= Woods| first= Charlotte Rene| title= Virginia becomes first Southern state to mandate paid family and medical leave for workers |url= https://virginiamercury.com/2026/05/12/virginia-becomes-first-southern-state-to-mandate-paid-family-and-medical-leave-for-workers/ |access-date= May 12, 2026| website= [[Virginia Mercury]]|date= May 12, 2026}}</ref> It guarantees that employers pay at least $1,500 in wages per quarter; provide up to 12 weeks of paid [[family leave]] to enrolled employees to take care of a relative or a child or 12 weeks of safety service leave for victims or relatives of victims of domestic violence, harassment, sexual assault, or stalking; and replace at least 80% of the claimant's weekly wages, up to 100% of the statewide average weekly net earnings, or $1,507.<ref>{{Cite web| last1= Anderson| first1= Matthew T.| last2= Chowhan|first2= Emily Kendall| title= Virginia Adopts Family Leave Insurance and Bans Non-Competes for Health Care Workers |url= https://www.williamsmullen.com/insights/news/legal-news/virginia-adopts-family-leave-insurance-and-bans-non-competes-health-care |access-date= May 12, 2026| website= Williams Mullen| date= April 27, 2026}}</ref> The program is funded by a [[trust fund]] financed by remittances from employers deducted from employee payrolls and, for employers of more than ten workers, employer contributions.<ref>{{Cite web| last1= Guyan|first1= Alyson J.| last2= Nieman| first2= Matthew F.| title= Virginia's Workplace Changes for Employers: Paid Family Leave| url= https://www.jacksonlewis.com/insights/virginias-workplace-changes-employers-paid-family-leave |access-date= May 12, 2026| website= Jackson Lewis| date= April 23, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title= Paid family & med. leave insurance program; definitions, notice requirements, civil action, report. |url= https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/SB2|website=Virginia General Assembly |access-date= May 12, 2026}}</ref>

On May 14, Spanberger vetoed SB378 and HB1263, [[collective bargaining]] legislation backed by the Virginia Service Employees International Union (SEIU) and various labor groups.<ref name= ":7">{{Cite web |last= Woods |first= Charlotte Rene |date= 2026-05-15 |title= Spanberger vetoes bills allowing public employees to collectively bargain working conditions, wages |url= https://virginiamercury.com/2026/05/15/spanberger-vetoes-bills-allowing-public-employees-to-collectively-bargain-working-condition-wages/ |access-date= 2026-05-27 |website= Virginia Mercury |language= en-US}}</ref> The legislation would have granted state and municipal employees collective bargaining rights, and was supported by all of the state legislature's Democrats. Spanberger proposed amendments that would have weakened the legislation by permitting rather than requiring state agencies to bargain with workers, exempting the state's Port Authority and universities from bargaining, and delaying the law's implementation until the end of her term.<ref name= ":8">{{Cite web |last= Meyerson |first= Harold |date= 2026-05-25 |title= How Gov. Spanberger Betrayed Virginia's Workers |url= https://prospect.org/2026/05/25/how-gov-spanberger-betrayed-virginias-workers/ |access-date= 2026-08-06 |website= The American Prospect |language= en-US}}</ref> When the General Assembly rejected these amendments, Spanberger vetoed the bills.<ref name= ":7" />

On May 20, Spanberger signed HB5, which mandates that private-sector employers and state and local governments give employees at least one hour of [[paid sick leave]] for every 30 hours worked, which is allowed to accrue to a maximum of 40 hours per year, unless the employer gives employees a more generous benefit. The law extends benefits to over 1.2 million uncovered workers.<ref>{{Cite web| last= Singer|first= Jeff| title= Gov. Spanberger signs paid sick leave bill into law| url= https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/05/20/gov-spanberger-signs-paid-sick-leave-bill-into-law/ |access-date= May 20, 2026| website= WDBJ7| date= May 20, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title= Gov. Spanberger signs legislation expanding paid sick leave to all Virginia workers |url= https://www.wsls.com/news/local/2026/05/20/gov-spanberger-signs-legislation-expanding-paid-sick-leave-to-all-virginia-workers/ |access-date= May 20, 2026| website= WSLS|date= May 20, 2026}}</ref>

On June 26, Spanberger signed legislation raising benefits for [[unemployment]] claims filed after July 5 of that year by 11.2%, from $430 to $478 per week.<ref>{{Cite web| last= Ramsey| first= Joe| title= Virginia boosts weekly unemployment benefits under new legislation |url= https://www.wusa9.com/article/money/business/virginia-weekly-unemployment-benefits-new-legislation-spanberger/65-345484e3-520b-4964-8be0-5913aefb2248 |website= WUSA9| date= June 25, 2026| access-date= June 26, 2026}}</ref>

==== Cannabis ====
HB542 was the state legislature's most recent effort to expand the commercial sale of [[recreational cannabis]]. It would have established a framework for the creation of a [[Cannabis retail outlet|cannabis retail market]] administered and regulated by the Virginia Cannabis Control Authority and partially liberalized certain restrictions on sales quantities.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mirshahi|first=Dean|title=Retail market for recreational weed passes Virginia General Assembly|url=https://www.vpm.org/generalassembly/2026-03-14/retail-market-recreational-cannabis-marijuana-thc-krizek-aird-spanberger|website=[[VPM]]|date=March 14, 2026|access-date=June 12, 2026}}</ref> Spanberger referred the bill back to the General Assembly with recommendations that would have delayed the retail market's launch by six months, increased civil penalties for illegal cannabis use to a class 4 misdemeanor, raised the [[excise tax]] on cannabis production to 8%, and reduced the number of cannabis dispensaries to be authorized by regulators to 200.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Angell|first=Tom|title=Virginia Governor Wants Amendments To Marijuana Sales Legalization Bill, Including Delayed Market Launch|url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/virginia-governor-wants-amendments-to-marijuana-sales-legalization-bill-including-delayed-market-launch/|website=Marijuana Moment|date=April 14, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Gov. Spanberger reviews plethora of legislation passed by General Assembly|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/04/14/gov-abigail-spanberger-reviews-plethora-legislation-passed-by-general-assembly/|website=WDBJ7|date=April 14, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref> The General Assembly rejected these recommendations.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Angell|first=Tom|title=Virginia Lawmakers Reject Governor's Amendments To Marijuana Sales Legalization Bill|url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/virginia-lawmakers-reject-governors-amendments-to-marijuana-sales-legalization-bill/|website=Marijuana Moment|date=April 22, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref>

As a result, Spanberger vetoed the bill on May 19, 2026,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mirshahi|first=Dean|title=Spanberger vetoes Virginia retail weed market bill, despite campaign pledge|url=https://www.vpm.org/generalassembly/2026-05-19/spanberger-retail-marijuana-market-veto-krizek-aird-higgs-wise|website=[[VPM]]|date=May 19, 2026|access-date=June 12, 2026}}</ref> despite supporting the creation of a retail cannabis market as long as it had strong labeling and regulation.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Capodanno|first=Kate|title=Spanberger talks future of cannabis in the Commonwealth|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2025/12/26/spanberger-talks-future-cannabis-commonwealth/|website=WDBJ4|date=December 26, 2025|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref> She cited the bill's "rushed timeframe", which she said gave the state too little time to design comprehensive strategies for regulating the market or to learn from the successes and errors of other states that liberalized cannabis retailing.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Englander|first=Tyler|title=Spanberger explains retail cannabis veto, stakeholders respond|url=https://www.wric.com/news/politics/capitol-connection/spanberger-explains-retail-cannabis-veto/|website=WRIC|date=May 20, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref> Negotiations continued in the following weeks, and they reached a compromise on June 16.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Englander|first=Tyler|title=Spanberger says conversations are ongoing about creating a legal recreational marijuana market|url=https://www.wric.com/news/politics/capitol-connection/spanberger-says-conversations-are-ongoing-about-creating-a-legal-recreational-marijuana-market/|website=WRIC|date=June 9, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Angell|first=Tom|title=Virginia Lawmakers And Governor Meet To Discuss Marijuana Sales Legalization Compromise That Could Pass This Month|url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/virginia-lawmakers-and-governor-meet-to-discuss-marijuana-sales-legalization-compromise-that-could-pass-this-month/|website=Marijuana Moment|date=June 5, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Banzouzi|first=Chrysnel|title=Gov. Spanberger announces compromise to open retail marijuana market in Virginia|url=https://www.wavy.com/news/politics/virginia-politics/gov-spanberger-announces-compromise-to-open-retail-marijuana-market-in-virginia/|website=WAVY|date=June 16, 2026|access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref>

Concurrently, Spanberger supported and signed legislation that strengthened cannabis users' [[parental rights]] by restricting authorities' power to infringe upon custody or visitation of a dependent child on the basis of cannabis possession, implementing protections for health professionals at [[hospice]]s, [[nursing home]]s and [[assisted living facilities]] that aid terminally ill patients in using [[medical cannabis]] treatment from punishment,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Angell|first=Tom|title=Virginia Governor Signs Bills To Protect Marijuana Users' Parental Rights And Allow Medical Use In Hospitals|url=https://www.marijuanamoment.net/virginia-governor-signs-bills-to-protect-marijuana-users-parental-rights-and-allow-medical-use-in-hospitals/|website=Marijuana Moment|date=April 13, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref> as well as a bill that reduces the sentences of people with cannabis use convictions for crimes that are no longer chargeable offenses under current law since the full legalization of cannabis use in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Krauss|first=Ethan|title=Gov. Spanberger signs marijuana resentencing bill into law|url=https://www.wavy.com/video/gov-spanberger-signs-marijuana-resentencing-bill-into-law/|website=WAVY|date=May 15, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Moore|first=Colby|title=Virginia opens the door for resentencing marijuana convictions|url=https://www.wboc.com/news/virginia-opens-the-door-for-resentencing-marijuana-convictions/article_b4310b7a-59b6-476a-8f67-3a4af3bd3b64.html|website=WBOC|date=May 15, 2026|access-date=June 10, 2026}}</ref>

==== Gun control ====
On May 14, 2026, Spanberger signed an "[[assault weapons]]" ban, making it a class 1 misdemeanor to import, sell, manufacture, purchase, or transfer certain [[semi-automatic pistol]]s, [[Semi-automatic shotgun|shotguns]] and [[high-capacity magazine]]s after July 1, 2026, and defines penalties for breaching this law, with exceptions for law enforcement officers and members of the [[United States Armed Forces]]. Semi-automatic shotguns for hunting are not considered assault weapons, and semi-automatic firearms owned before July 1 may be retained.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Virginia Governor Spanberger signs assault weapons ban into law|url=https://wset.com/news/local/virginia-governor-spanberger-signs-assault-weapons-ban-into-law-firearms-high-capacity-magazines-may-2026|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=WSET|date=May 14, 2026|last=Irby|first=Sarah|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Spanberger amends, signs sweeping gun legislation reshaping Virginia's firearm laws|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/14/spanberger-amends-signs-sweeping-gun-legislation-reshaping-virginias-firearm-laws/|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=Mintz|date=April 14, 2026|last=Schmidt|first=Markus|language=en-US}}</ref> The law was subject to litigation immediately after being enacted.<ref>{{Cite web|title=NRA suing Gov. Spanberger following signing of 'assault weapons ban'|url=https://wcyb.com/news/local/nra-suing-gov-spanberger-following-signing-of-assault-weapons-ban-magazines-second-amendment-supreme-court-national-rifle-association|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=WCYB|date=May 15, 2026|language=en-US}}</ref>

In April, Spanberger signed bills strengthening regulations requiring firearms sellers to prevent the illegal distribution of weapons to arms traffickers, [[straw purchasers]], or people forbidden from owning a gun under state or federal law;<ref>{{Cite web|title=Spanberger signs some gun bills into law, while others wait with looming deadline|url=https://wset.com/news/local/governor-abigail-spanberger-signs-one-gun-bill-into-law-while-others-wait-with-looming-deadline-hb21-hb217-hb1525-firearms-virginia-april-2026|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=WSET|date=April 13, 2026|last=Mullet|first=Allison|language=en-US}}</ref> prohibiting the carry of firearms or explosive material on college campuses or in the vicinity of public educational institutions, with exemptions for [[Reserve Officers' Training Corps]] cadets, military personnel, and law enforcement;<ref>{{Cite web|title=Spanberger signs bills to ban firearms at Virginia's public colleges, universities|url=https://www.wric.com/news/politics/capitol-connection/spanberger-signs-firearm-bans-college-universities/|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=WRIC|last=Roever|first=Alex|date=May 12, 2026|language=en-US}}</ref> and raising the minimum age to purchase a gun to 21.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Firearms, certain; definitions, possession, transportation, or purchase by certain persons, penalty.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB1525/|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=Virginia General Assembly|language=en-US}}</ref> She signed other restrictions relating to the possession of firearms in hospitals, the presence of [[polling place]]s, and laws relating to gun owners' responsibility to keep their firearms secure.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mascia|first=Jennifer|title=Democrats in Virginia Just Pushed 25 Gun Reforms to the Finish Line|url=https://www.thetrace.org/2026/03/virginia-25-gun-reforms-spanberger/|access-date=May 16, 2026|website=[[The Trace (website)|The Trace]]|date=March 24, 2026|language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Election integrity ====
On March 24, 2026, Spanberger issued an executive order authorizing Virginia's reentry into the [[Electronic Registration Information Center]], a nonprofit, nonpartisan organization with 26 member states that share voter registration and identification data to better keep accurate [[voter roll]]s, from which Governor Youngkin withdrew the state in 2023. The executive order requires the [[Virginia Department of Motor Vehicles]] to coordinate with the [[Virginia Department of Elections|Department of Elections]] to conduct thorough voter roll maintenance operations, prohibits the purge of ineligible voters from the rolls within 90 days of a primary or general election, requires local registrars to ascertain reported election results' integrity after each election, and directs the Commissioner of the Department of Elections to verify that ballot chain of custody procedures are followed and [[Electronic voting machine|voting equipment]] is certified according to federal standards, properly tested before each election, and never connected to the Internet.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Game|first=Colton|title=Gov. Spanberger signs executive order aimed at protecting election integrity, rejoins ERIC|url=https://www.wsls.com/news/local/2026/03/24/gov-spanberger-signs-executive-order-aimed-at-protecting-election-integrity-rejoins-eric/|date=March 24, 2026|access-date=May 8, 2026|website=WSLS|language=en|archive-date=March 25, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260325015042/https://www.wsls.com/news/local/2026/03/24/gov-spanberger-signs-executive-order-aimed-at-protecting-election-integrity-rejoins-eric/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==== 2026 Virginia redistricting amendment ====
{{Main|2026 Virginia redistricting amendment|2025-2026 United States redistricting}}

Until 2020, the General Assembly was responsible for delineating the boundaries of congressional districts, but this process was amended by a [[2020 Virginia Question 1|ballot measure]] approved by voters on November 3, 2020, which referred redistricting authority from the state legislature to a bipartisan redistricting commission staffed by eight legislators and eight citizens and tied the redistricting process to the decennial [[United States Census|census]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://scholarship.richmond.edu/lawreview/vol55/iss1/10/|title=Readying Virginia for Redistricting After a Decade of Election Law Upheaval|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=University of Richmond Law Review|last=Chambers Jr.|first=Henry L.}}</ref> Bypassing the commission would require another amendment to the [[Constitution of Virginia]]. The General Assembly can pass amendments through a majority vote by the House of Delegates and the Senate in two consecutive legislative sessions, which would send the amendment to be approved by the voters as a ballot measure.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://amendments.rmc.edu/amending/|title=Amending the State Constitution|date=April 7, 2022|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[Randolph-Macon College]]}}</ref>

Despite Spanberger's August 2025 pledge to make no attempt to redraw Virginia's congressional map if elected,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Harrell|first=Princess|title=Gov. Moore eyes Maryland map redraw; Spanberger says no changes planned in Virginia|url=https://wjla.com/news/elections/wes-moore-abigail-spanberger-redrawing-maryland-congressional-districts-map-virginia-election-2025-governor-race-redistricting-winsome-earle-sears-texas-gerrymandering-florida-missouri-indiana-andy-harris|date=August 25, 2025|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=WJLA|language=en|archive-date=August 26, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250826005012/https://wjla.com/news/elections/wes-moore-abigail-spanberger-redrawing-maryland-congressional-districts-map-virginia-election-2025-governor-race-redistricting-winsome-earle-sears-texas-gerrymandering-florida-missouri-indiana-andy-harrisn|url-status=live}}</ref> in late October state Democratic leaders announced a plan to redraw the state's map through a constitutional amendment that would temporarily suspend the bipartisan commission's authority to permit a one-time mid-decade redistricting session,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Epstein|first=Reid J.|title=Virginia Democrats Plan to Redraw House Maps in Redistricting Push|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/23/us/politics/virginia-democrats-redistrict.html|date=October 23, 2025|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[The New York Times]]|language=en|archive-date=November 2, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251102051916/https://www.nytimes.com/2025/10/23/us/politics/virginia-democrats-redistrict.html|url-status=live}}</ref> which she said she would not oppose.<ref>{{Cite web|last1=Peoples|first1=Steve|last2=Diaz|first2=Olivia|title=Democrat Abigail Spanberger backs Virginia legislature's redistricting push|url=https://apnews.com/article/virginia-redistricting-spanberger-democrats-congress-40f30039fb97839ce8c50bdfff759bb1|date=October 27, 2025|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[Associated Press]]|language=en|archive-date=November 4, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251104053703/https://apnews.com/article/virginia-redistricting-spanberger-democrats-congress-40f30039fb97839ce8c50bdfff759bb1|url-status=live}}</ref> A corresponding resolution was adopted by the Assembly in October that allows the Virginia General Assembly to consider constitutional amendments related to redistricting during a special session,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Schmidt|first=Markus|date=October 27, 2025|title=Democrats push redistricting amendment as special session jolts Virginia ahead of election|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2025/10/27/democrats-push-redistricting-amendment-as-special-session-jolts-virginia-ahead-of-election/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251028232009/https://virginiamercury.com/2025/10/27/democrats-push-redistricting-amendment-as-special-session-jolts-virginia-ahead-of-election/|archive-date=October 28, 2025|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]}}</ref> which was agreed to by the House on the 29th<ref>{{Cite web|last=Rice|first=Jen|date=October 29, 2025|title=Virginia House Advances Democratic Plan to Counter GOP Gerrymandering|url=https://www.democracydocket.com/news-alerts/virginia-house-advances-democratic-plan-to-counter-gop-gerrymandering/|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260225022811/https://www.democracydocket.com/news-alerts/virginia-house-advances-democratic-plan-to-counter-gop-gerrymandering/|archive-date=February 25, 2026|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[Democracy Docket]]}}</ref> and by the Senate on the 31st.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Khalil|first=Jahd|date=October 31, 2025|title=Virginia Senate votes to propose redistricting amendment|url=https://www.vpm.org/generalassembly/2025-10-31/ga-hj6007-gerrymandering-senate-favola-stanley-vanvalkenburg-obenshain|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251031224927/https://www.vpm.org/generalassembly/2025-10-31/ga-hj6007-gerrymandering-senate-favola-stanley-vanvalkenburg-obenshain|archive-date=October 31, 2025|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[VPM]]}}</ref> Both floors reapproved the amendment after the beginning of [[164th Virginia General Assembly|164th General Assembly]],<ref>{{Cite web|last=Beyer|first=Elizabeth|date=January 27, 2026|title=Virginia's congressional redistricting effort on hold after Tazewell court ruling|url=https://cardinalnews.org/2026/01/27/virginias-congressional-redistricting-effort-on-hold-after-tazewell-court-ruling/|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=Cardinal News|language=en-US}}</ref> and Spanberger signed the legislation that sent it to the voters.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Beyer|first=Elizabeth|date=February 7, 2026|title=Gov. Spanberger signs bill to enable redistricting referendum|url=https://cardinalnews.org/2026/02/06/gov-spanberger-signs-bill-to-enable-redistricting-referendum/|access-date=March 20, 2026|website=Cardinal News|language=en-US}}</ref>

The amendment was ultimately approved, 51.69% to 48.31%.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 22, 2026|title=Live Results: Virginia approves redistricting plan in special election|url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/live-results-virginia-redistricting-special-election|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[PBS]]|language=en-US}}</ref> It was struck down by the [[Supreme Court of Virginia]] on May 8,<ref>{{cite web|last1=Lieb|first1=David A.|last2=Mulvihill|first2=Geoff|title=Virginia Supreme Court strikes down Democrats' redrawn US House maps, giving Republicans a win|url=https://apnews.com/article/redistricting-virginia-congress-democrats-republicans-12a31037f3c9a94d3cb9fbcaaf84d94f|access-date=May 8, 2026|website=[[Associated Press]]|date=May 8, 2026}}</ref> a ruling appealed by state Democrats.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Wilder|first=Drew|date=May 8, 2026|title=Virginia Democrats to appeal ruling against redistricting to U.S. Supreme Court |url=https://www.nbcwashington.com/news/local/virginia-democrats-appeal-ruling-redistricting-us-supreme-court/4102136/?amp=1|access-date=May 9, 2026|website=NBC Washington|language=en}}</ref> The U.S. Supreme Court rejected the appeal on May 15.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Groppe|first=Maureen|date=May 15, 2026|title=Democrats' redistricting push in Virginia dies at Supreme Court |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/politics/2026/05/15/virginia-democrats-redistricting-supreme-court/90064228007/|access-date=May 17, 2026|website=USA Today|language=en}}</ref>

==== Other actions ====
On February 24, 2026, Spanberger delivered the [[Response to the State of the Union address|Democratic response]] to Trump's [[2026 State of the Union Address]] from the chamber of the [[House of Burgesses]] in the reconstructed [[Capitol (Williamsburg, Virginia)|Capitol]] in [[Williamsburg, Virginia]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-02-19 |title=Virginia Gov. Abigail Spanberger to deliver Democratic response to Trump's State of the Union - CBS News |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/abigail-spanberger-trump-state-of-the-union-democratic-response/ |access-date=2026-02-24 |website=www.cbsnews.com |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Karni |first=Annie |date=2026-02-19 |title=Democrats Choose Spanberger to Deliver State of the Union Response to Trump |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/19/us/politics/abigail-spanberger-democrats-state-of-the-union-response-trump.html |access-date=2026-02-24 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.wtkr.com/news/politics/he-lied-spanberger-sharply-critiques-trumps-first-year-in-democratic-rebuttal-to-state-of-the-union|title='He lied:' Spanberger sharply critiques Trump's first year in Democratic rebuttal to State of the Union|publisher=[[WTKR]]|date=February 24, 2026|access-date=February 25, 2026|website=wtkr.com}}</ref> In February 2026, Spanberger signed legislation sending three constitutional amendments to voters for referendums later in 2026: again, the [[2026 Virginia Right to Reproductive Freedom Amendment|Right to Reproductive Freedom Amendment]], enshrining reproductive rights, the [[2026 Virginia Repeal Same-Sex Marriage Ban Amendment|Repeal Same-Sex Marriage Ban Amendment]], which rescinds the longstanding state same-sex marriage ban that will go into effect if ''[[Obergefell v. Hodges]]'' is overturned, and the [[2026 Virginia Voting Rights Restoration Amendment|Voting Rights Restoration Amendment]], which restores voting rights to formerly incarcerated people who completed felony sentences.<ref name="amend-cite" />

On March 9, 2026, her 50th day in office, Spanberger highlighted $575 million in new business investments (including the second-largest economic development deal in Southern Virginia history, creating nearly 2,000 jobs), progress on lowering costs through the Affordable Virginia Agenda and day-one executive orders, and the launch of a cross-Commonwealth school listening tour.<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 9, 2026 |title=Governor Spanberger Marks 50 Days in Office: $575 Million in New Business Investment, Lowering Costs, & Strengthening Schools |url=https://www.governor.virginia.gov/newsroom/news-releases/2026/march-releases/name-1114352-en.html |access-date=16 March 2026 |website=Office of the Governor of Virginia}}</ref>

The 2026 legislative session concluded on March 14 with sine die adjournment without a final biennial budget agreement, due to a dispute over data center tax exemptions.<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 23, 2026|title=Virginia lawmakers recess special session without a budget deal|url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/23/virginia-lawmakers-adjourn-special-session-without-a-budget-deal/|access-date=May 1, 2026|website=[[Virginia Mercury]]|last1=Schmidt|first1=Markus|last2=Heckt|first2=Shannon|last3=Woods|first3=Charlotte Rene|archive-date=April 26, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260426093947/https://virginiamercury.com/2026/04/23/virginia-lawmakers-adjourn-special-session-without-a-budget-deal/}}</ref>

In early April, Spanberger signed bipartisan legislation establishing a High School Certified Nurse Aide Training and Certification Program, administered by the [[Virginia Board of Education|Board of Education]] and Virginia Board of Nursing for the purpose of creating a pathway for qualified high school students to prepare for and obtain certification as a certified [[nurse aide]];<ref>{{Cite web|title=High School Certified Nurse Aide Training/Certification Program; established, requirements, report.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB413|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 12, 2026}}</ref> allowing high school students to serve in an [[apprenticeship]] program relating to the [[culinary arts]] or [[information technology]];<ref>{{Cite web|title=Employment prohibition exceptions; apprenticeship program for children 16 years of age or older.|url=https://lis.virginia.gov/bill-details/20261/HB275|website=Virginia General Assembly|access-date=May 12, 2026}}</ref> a schoolground cellphone ban;<ref>{{Cite web|last=Buzbee|first=John|title=Spanberger signs into law slate of education bills, including prohibition of cellphone use in school|url=https://www.pilotonline.com/2026/04/08/laws-signed-education/|website=The Virginian Pilot|date=April 8, 2026|access-date=May 16, 2026}}</ref> and a bill that codifies and expands statewide the [[Roanoke, Virginia|Roanoke]] Community Builders Pilot Program that seeks to reduce youth violence by providing educational opportunities for secondary-school-aged children.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Freund|first=Jason|title=Governor Abigail Spanberger signs bill that puts Community Builders Program permanently in place|url=https://www.wsls.com/news/local/2026/04/06/governor-abigail-spanberger-signs-bill-that-puts-community-builders-program-permanently-in-place/|website=WSLS|date=April 6, 2026|access-date=May 16, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Governor Abigail Spanberger marks 100th Day with Bipartisan Bill Signing at Roanoke Higher Education Center|url=https://www.roanokerambler.com/governor-abigail-spanberger-marks-100th-day-with-bipartisan-bill-signing-at-roanoke-higher-education-center/|website=Roanoke Rambler|date=April 29, 2026|access-date=May 16, 2026}}</ref>

In April 2026, Spanberger signed a bill that eliminates tax breaks for organizations related to the [[Confederate States of America]],<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 14, 2026|last=Venarchik|first=Anna|title=Virginia Governor Ends Tax Breaks for Confederate Groups|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/14/us/virginia-tax-breaks-confederate.html|access-date=May 7, 2026|website=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> and a package of legislation relating to [[maternal healthcare]] and [[telemedicine]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=April 22, 2026|last=Singer|first=Jeff|title=Gov. Spanberger signs "momnibus" bills to overhaul & improve maternal health care coverage|url=https://www.wdbj7.com/2026/04/22/gov-spanberger-signs-momnibus-bills-overhaul-improve-maternal-health-care-coverage/|access-date=May 10, 2026|website=WDBJ4}}</ref> Other major bills passed and sent to Spanberger's desk include the legalization of adult-use cannabis sales (effective January 1, 2027), regulation of "skill" games, authorization of collective bargaining for public employees, and a sweeping gun-safety package (including an assault weapons ban, large-capacity magazine restrictions, and expanded industry liability). Most bills would take effect on July 1, 2026.<ref name="ReferenceA"/><ref>{{Cite news |date=March 14, 2026 |title=The 10 most important things that happened in Virginia's 2026 legislative session |url=https://virginiamercury.com/2026/03/14/the-10-most-important-things-that-happened-in-virginias-2026-legislative-session/ |access-date=16 March 2026 |website=Virginia Mercury}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=March 14, 2026 |title=The 2026 General Assembly session wraps with no budget, special session on the horizon |url=https://cardinalnews.org/2026/03/14/the-2026-general-assembly-session-wraps-with-no-budget-special-session-on-the-horizon/ |access-date=16 March 2026 |website=Cardinal News}}</ref>

In July 2026, Spanberger ended a rulemaking process that would have continued the process of banning transgender women from competing in women's sports in Virginia and entering certain female restrooms and locker rooms.<ref>{{Cite web |last=News |first=Latest & Breaking News on Fox |title=Virginia Governor Abigail Spanberger quietly rolls back Youngkin's plans to protect girls' sports |url=https://www.wfmd.com/2026/09/17/virginia-governor-abigail-spanberger-quietly-rolls-back-youngkins-plans-to-protect-girls-sports/ |access-date=2026-09-22 |website=930 WFMD Free Talk |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Minock |first=Nick |date=2026-09-16 |title=Spanberger ends Youngkin-era rulemaking on transgender sports |url=https://wjla.com/news/local/virginia-abigail-spanberger-ends-glenn-youngkin-era-rule-on-transgender-sports-womens-sport-biological-males-competing-locker-rooms-safe-spaces-regulations |access-date=2026-09-22 |website=WJLA |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Stanbridge |first=Alexia |date=2025-09-24 |title=Spanberger addresses stance on transgender women in sports and bathrooms |url=https://wset.com/news/local/spanberger-addresses-stance-on-transgender-women-in-sports-and-bathrooms-democratic-party-virginia-gubernatorial-candidate-election-day-2025 |access-date=2026-09-22 |website=WSET |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kutner |first=Brad |date=2026-09-14 |title=Spanberger quietly kills Youngkin-era transgender rulemaking effort |url=https://www.wvtf.org/news/2026-09-14/spanberger-quietly-kills-youngkin-era-transgender-rulemaking-effort |access-date=2026-09-22 |website=WVTF |language=en}}</ref>

=== Public image ===
Very early in Spanberger's governorship, opinion polls showed that her job approval rating was fairly stable and similar to the average of previous Virginia governors. A poll by the Institute for Policy and Opinion Research at [[Roanoke College]] in early February showed that 53% of Virginians gave her high marks for her job performance, while 38% disapproved.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Jarvis|first=Brandon|date=February 23, 2026|title=New polling on Spanberger, redistricting and guns|url=https://www.virginiascope.com/new-polling-on-spanberger-redistricting-and-guns/|access-date=May 10, 2026|website=Virginia Scope}}</ref>

By April, her approval rating had dropped significantly, with a ''[[Washington Post]]''–[[Schar School]] poll finding that 47% of voters approved of Spanberger and 46% disapproved. This marked the worst net approval rating for a governor in the early months of their term in ''Washington Post'' polls since the early 1990s.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schneider |first=Gregory S.|date=6 April 2026 |title=Virginians deeply divided on Spanberger months after her landslide win |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/04/06/virginia-spanberger-approval-poll-affordability/ |access-date=6 April 2026 |website=Washington Post}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Minock |first=Nick |date=2026-04-06 |title=Virginia Gov. Abigail Spanberger's approval rating tanks in new GMU, Washington Post poll |url=https://abcnews4.com/news/nation-world/virginia-gov-abigail-spanbergers-approval-rating-drop-gmu-washington-post-poll-immigration-affordability-taxes-voters-redistricting |access-date=2026-08-12 |website=WCIV |language=en}}</ref> Similarly, a State Navigate poll conducted on April 10–13 showed her net approval rating to be breaking even.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Virginia Statewide Poll Topline|url=https://statenavigate.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/VA-Redistricting-Topline.pdf|access-date=May 10, 2026|website=State Navigate}}</ref> But after the 2026 Virginia redistricting amendment, a ''Public Sentiment Institute'' poll found that 52% of voters approved of Spanberger and 41% disapproved, as in earlier polls.<ref>{{cite web|title=The Virginia Project/The Public Sentiment Institute - NEW Virginia Poll Shows Democrats' Advantage Across The State|date=May 5, 2026|url=https://tpsiofficial.substack.com/p/the-virginia-project-the-public-sentiment|access-date=May 9, 2026|work=The Public Sentiment Institute}}</ref> Her net approval rating fell back to -2% by July, according to a poll by [[Virginia Commonwealth University]].<ref>{{cite web |title= VCU poll: Voters like Spanberger's key policies but are mixed on the governor herself. Here's why. |date= July 16, 2026 |url= https://cardinalnews.org/2026/07/16/vcu-poll-voters-like-spanbergers-key-policies-but-are-mixed-on-the-governor-herself-heres-why/ |access-date= July 17, 2026 |last= Yancey |first= Dwayne |website= Cardinal News}}</ref>

In June 2026, [[Vox (website)|''Vox'']] described Spanberger as polarizing, attempting to placate both businesses and Democrats, while being viewed negatively by Republicans.<ref>{{Cite web |url= https://www.vox.com/politics/490493/abigail-spanberger-vetoes-collective-bargaining-marijuana |title= This Democratic governor won in a landslide — and is now at war with her own base |website=Vox |date= June 2, 2026 |first1= Andrew|last1= Prokop |access-date= June 5, 2026}}</ref>

==Political positions==
{{Modern liberalism in the United States sidebar|expanded=politicians}}
Spanberger positions herself as a [[New Democrats (United States)|moderate Democrat]]<ref name="centrist"><!-- She is described as a centrist by multiple reliable sources -->{{cite news|last=Pope|first=Michael|date=June 13, 2018|title=Democrats Didn't Always Pick The Most Progressive Candidate And That Might Help Them In November|work=WVTF|url=https://www.wvtf.org/post/democrats-didnt-always-pick-most-progressive-candidate-and-might-help-them-november|access-date=August 27, 2019}}
<br>{{cite web|title=The moderate squad: swing-state Democrats wary of leftward path|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/jul/20/the-squad-alexandria-ocasio-cortez-ilhan-omar-democrats-trump|last=Gambino|first=Lauren|date=July 20, 2019|access-date=August 27, 2019|newspaper=The Guardian}}
<br>{{Cite news |last=Greve |first=Joan E. |date=2023-11-03 |title=Abigail Spanberger flexes her political power in a battleground state: 'I could see her as president' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/nov/03/who-is-abigail-spanberger-virginia-congress-democrat |access-date=2024-09-21 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}
<br>{{Cite news |last=Rubin |first=Jennifer |date=2022-11-09 |title=Opinion: Abigail Spanberger again shows how to win as a centrist Democrat |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2022/11/09/spanberger-virginia-win-centrist-democrat/ |access-date=2024-09-21 |newspaper=Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}<br>{{Cite news |last=Mutnick |first=Ally |date=July 28, 2023 |title=Abigail Spanberger tells Democrats she will run for governor |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/07/28/spanberger-future-00108756 |work=Politico}}
<br>{{Cite news |last=Broadwater |first=Luke |date=November 12, 2023 |title=Spanberger Announces Run for Governor in Virginia, Leaving House Seat in Play |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/11/13/us/politics/abigail-spanberger-virginia-governor.html |work=The New York Times}}
<br>{{Cite news |last=Peterson |first=Kristina |date=September 14, 2021 |title=Democrats Hear Conflicting Messages From Voters on $3.5 Trillion Plan |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-hear-conflicting-messages-from-voters-on-3-5-trillion-plan-11631622702 |work=The Wall Street Journal}}</ref> and has called herself a "passionate pragmatist".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Vozzella |first1=Laura |title=Spanberger faces reckoning with left in bid for Va. governor |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/12/26/spanberger-virginia-governor-democrats-trump/ |access-date=December 26, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=December 26, 2024}}</ref> Virginia [[NPR]] affiliate [[WCVE-FM]] called Spanberger's legislative voting record "typical in this highly partisan era" and said she has always voted for Biden's agenda while still being the fifth-most bipartisan House member when it came to cosponsoring legislation and opposing one of Biden's [[executive order]]s on immigration.<ref>{{cite web |title=PolitiFact VA: Spanberger backs Biden in Congress, but they don't agree on every issue |url=https://www.vpm.org/news/2022-08-10/politifact-va-spanberger-backs-biden-in-congress-but-they-dont-agree-on-every |website=[[WCVE-FM]] |date=August 10, 2022 |publisher=[[VPM Media Corporation]] |access-date=January 10, 2025}}</ref> In the [[2019 Speaker of the United States House of Representatives election|2019 speaker of the United States House of Representatives election]] on the opening day of the [[116th United States Congress]], Spanberger voted for Representative [[Cheri Bustos]], an Illinois Democrat, joining 11 other Democrats who did not back [[Nancy Pelosi]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Virginia congresswoman one of 12 Democrats to oppose Pelosi's bid for speaker |url=https://wtkr.com/2019/01/04/virginia-congresswoman-one-of-12-democrats-to-oppose-pelosis-bid-for-speaker/ |website=Channel 3, WTKR |date=January 4, 2019 |publisher=Tribune Broadcasting |access-date=January 18, 2019}}</ref>

===Abortion===
Spanberger supports [[United States abortion-rights movement|abortion rights]].<ref name="RTD10272018">{{cite web |url=https://www.richmond.com/news/virginia/government-politics/a-look-at-where-brat-and-spanberger-stand-on-the/article_d92a8931-cc2d-5493-ae86-8fe96dcdfcde.html |title=A look at where Brat and Spanberger stand on the issues |last=Wilson |first=Patrick |date=October 27, 2018 |newspaper=Richmond Times-Dispatch |access-date=September 28, 2019}}</ref> She opposed the U.S. Supreme Court's decision in ''[[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization]]'', overturning the right to abortion established by ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'', saying that "it undermines the right to privacy and a woman's right to choose".<ref>{{cite news|url = https://www.insidenova.com/culpeper/spanberger-releases-statement-on-roe-v-wade-being-overturned/article_83c360d2-f3cb-11ec-996e-cbe74f50f46e.html|title =Spanberger releases statement on Roe v. Wade being overturned|date = June 24, 2022|work = [[Culpeper Times]]|access-date = December 17, 2025}}</ref> She opposes legislation to restrict abortion, saying that the government should not "mandate a pregnancy".<ref>{{cite web|last=Rankin|first=Sarah|date=November 3, 2022|title=Spanberger, Vega in tight race for Virginia's 7th district|website=Associated Press|url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-abortion-biden-inflation-race-and-ethnicity-b457fbe13bb8ea314fb582b7c69d56d8#|access-date=April 2, 2025}}</ref> During her gubernatorial campaign, Spanberger said she would support a constitutional amendment to restore "the ''[[Roe v. Wade|Roe]]'' standard" and that she supported Virginia's existing laws requiring minors seeking abortions to receive parental consent, and certain limitations on third-trimester abortions.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.pbs.org/newshour/politics/republican-winsome-earle-sears-and-democrat-abigail-spanberger-spar-over-abortion-in-virginia|title=Republican Winsome Earle-Sears and Democrat Abigail Spanberger spar over abortion in Virginia|publisher=[[PBS]]|website=pbs.org|date=October 20, 2025|access-date=November 4, 2025|first1=Olivia|last1=Diaz|first2=Bill|last2=Barrow}}</ref>

=== COVID-19 ===
In February 2023, Spanberger was one of 12 House Democrats who voted in favor of H.J. Res. 7, a Republican-sponsored resolution to terminate the national emergency concerning COVID-19 declared in March 2020.<ref>{{cite news |last=Schnell |first=Mychael |date=February 1, 2023 |title=House passes resolution to end COVID-19 national emergency |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/3839808-house-gop-passes-bill-to-end-covid-19-national-emergency/ |work=The Hill |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=H.J. Res. 7 – Relating to a national emergency declared by the President on March 13, 2020 |url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/118th-congress/house-joint-resolution/7 |publisher=Congress.gov |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref>

During her 2025 campaign for governor of Virginia, a January 6, 2021, photograph of Spanberger wearing an emergency escape hood inside the Capitol was circulated online by critics, who misrepresented it as COVID-19 protective gear and mocked her.<ref>{{cite news |title=Attack Against Abigail Spanberger Backfires With January 6th Reminder |url=https://meidasnews.com/news/attack-against-abigail-spanberger-backfires-with-january-6th-reminder |website= |date=August 19, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Photo of Abigail Spanberger's 'COVID-19 Hood' go viral as internet explodes with laughter and outrage |url=https://www.shorenewsnetwork.com/photo-of-abigail-spanbergers-covid-19-hood-go-viral-as-internet-explodes-with-laughter-and-outrage |website=Shore News Network |date=October 12, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref> The hoods, which protect against smoke and chemical irritants, were distributed to members of Congress by Capitol Police during the attack on the Capitol; the misrepresentation was widely noted and debunked online, with some observers saying that the circulation of the image drew attention to the events of January 6.<ref>{{cite news |title=Attack Against Abigail Spanberger Backfires With January 6th Reminder |url=https://meidasnews.com/news/attack-against-abigail-spanberger-backfires-with-january-6th-reminder |website=MeidasTouch Network |date=August 19, 2025 |access-date=January 29, 2026}}</ref>

=== Criminal justice ===
In 2023, Spanberger voted against [[DC Home Rule Act|overturning]] the [[Washington, D.C.|District of Columbia]]'s revision of its criminal code, which reduced the maximum penalties for burglary, carjacking, and robbery. Virginia Attorney General [[Jason Miyares]] said, "This is one of the most extreme criminal-first, victim-last measures we've seen across the nation."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Minock |first=Nick |date=March 6, 2023 |title=Va. AG slams Spanberger, Wexton, Beyer and Connolly for approving DC criminal code bill |url=https://wjla.com/news/local/dc-criminal-code-bill-legislation-senate-decision-virginia-attorney-general-jason-miyares-response-congress-spanberger-wexton-beyer-conolly-approval-joe-biden-house-senate-reccomendation |access-date=April 4, 2023 |website=WJLA}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=February 9, 2023 |title=House votes to overturn DC criminal code and voting laws |url=https://apnews.com/article/politics-united-states-government-house-of-representatives-district-columbia-feb0bf10eba51093132b67c0268fcff1 |access-date=April 4, 2023 |website=AP NEWS}}</ref>

Spanberger opposes [[Defund the police|defunding the police]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Flynn |first=Meagan |date=November 11, 2020 |title=Spanberger sparked a debate about 'defund the police' attacks. Cameron Webb slogged through them. |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/webb-spanberger-defund-attacks/2020/11/11/9afd6408-2426-11eb-952e-0c475972cfc0_story.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230503043738/https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/webb-spanberger-defund-attacks/2020/11/11/9afd6408-2426-11eb-952e-0c475972cfc0_story.html |archive-date=May 3, 2023 |access-date=August 15, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref> and has supported bills that would increase the ability of local police departments to hire and train more officers.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Flynn |first=Meagan |date=August 20, 2022 |title=Spanberger flips the script on policing in race against auxiliary deputy |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/08/21/virginia-7th-policing-spanberger-vega/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230602062528/https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/08/21/virginia-7th-policing-spanberger-vega/ |archive-date=June 2, 2023 |access-date=August 15, 2024 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]}}</ref>

===Economy===
Although she was not a member of Congress when it passed, Spanberger criticized the 2017 [[Tax Cuts and Jobs Act]] supported by President [[Donald Trump]], arguing that its permanent tax cuts for corporations would increase the national debt.<ref name="RTD10272018" />

Spanberger called for the passage of the [[United States–Mexico–Canada Agreement|USMCA]] trade deal negotiated by the Trump administration, Mexico, and Canada.<ref>{{cite press release|url=https://spanberger.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=2276|title=Spanberger Pushes for Final Progress on USMCA: "We Need to Get this Done"|publisher=spanberger.house.gov | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200921223338/https://spanberger.house.gov/news/documentsingle.aspx?DocumentID=2276 |archive-date=September 21, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2019/08/31/democrats-trade-trump-1691486|title=Why these Democrats want to make a trade deal with Trump|publisher=Politico}}</ref> Spanberger opposed [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|Trump's tariffs]] during her 2025 gubernatorial campaign.<ref name="13 reasons"/> She has promoted [[free trade]] during her tenure as governor.<ref name="Trade Accelerator"/><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.governor.virginia.gov/newsroom/news-releases/2026/february-/name-1112932-en.html|title=Governor Spanberger Statement on President Trump's Tariffs|date=February 20, 2026|access-date=February 23, 2026|first1=Abigail|last1=Spanberger|website=Governor of Virginia}}</ref>

In May 2020, Spanberger voted against the [[HEROES Act]], a proposed $3 trillion stimulus package in response to the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite news|last=Flynn|first=Meagan|title=In a historically Republican stronghold, Democrat Abigail Spanberger looks to hang on|language=en-US|newspaper=The Washington Post|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/spanberger-freitas-7th-district/2020/09/11/786c30a8-eead-11ea-b4bc-3a2098fc73d4_story.html|access-date=November 28, 2020|issn=0190-8286}}</ref> She said the bill went "far beyond" pandemic relief and had no chance of passing the Republican-controlled [[United States Senate|Senate]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mattingly|first=Justin|title=Spanberger opposes latest stimulus package, saying it 'goes far beyond pandemic relief'|url=https://richmond.com/news/virginia/spanberger-opposes-latest-stimulus-package-saying-it-goes-far-beyond-pandemic-relief/article_c0d7b166-59aa-5204-b2c3-21d85fa5b3ab.html|access-date=November 28, 2020|website=Richmond Times-Dispatch|date=May 15, 2020 |language=en}}</ref> In November 2020, Spanberger led a bipartisan effort to secure the [[340B Drug Pricing Program]] against changes that would lead to significant increases in prescription medication costs.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web|date=November 15, 2020|title=Spanberger leads bipartisan effort to save 340B Drug Pricing Program|url=https://augustafreepress.com/spanberger-leads-bipartisan-effort-to-save-340b-drug-pricing-program/|access-date=November 28, 2020|website=Augusta Free Press|language=en |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20201115150716/https://augustafreepress.com/spanberger-leads-bipartisan-effort-to-save-340b-drug-pricing-program/ |archive-date=November 15, 2020}}</ref>

{{anchor|TRUST in Congress Act}}Spanberger supports banning members of Congress from trading stocks. She has introduced legislation that would require lawmakers, as well as their spouses and dependent children, to place assets in a [[blind trust]] while in office.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/house/586499-pelosi-faces-pushback-over-stock-trade-defense|title=Pelosi faces pushback over stock trade defense|access-date=January 11, 2022|date=December 21, 2021|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|author=Christina Marcos}}</ref><ref>Karl Evers-Hillstrom. (May 11, 2023). "Lawmakers call for markup on stock trading ban before August recess". [https://thehill.com/homenews/house/3999599-lawmakers-call-for-markup-on-stock-trading-ban-before-august-recess/ The Hill website] Retrieved May 11, 2023.</ref>

In September 2025, Spanberger wrote an opinion article in ''The Washington Post'' opposing [[Department of Government Efficiency|DOGE]] for conducting [[2025 United States federal mass layoffs|mass layoffs of federal workers in 2025]] which particularly affect Virginia, as many federal workers live there.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Spanberger |first1=Abigail |date=September 25, 2025 |title=Trump and DOGE created chaos that threatens Virginia's economy |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2025/09/25/abigail-spanberger-trump-doge-cuts-virginia-economy/ |access-date=September 25, 2025 |newspaper=Washington Post}}</ref> Spanberger focused her 2025 gubernatorial campaign on affordability and jobs.<ref name="13 reasons">{{Cite web |last=Yancey |first=Dwayne |date=5 November 2025 |title=13 reasons why Spanberger and the Democrats won so big. Trump is just one of them. |url=https://cardinalnews.org/2025/11/05/13-reasons-why-spanberger-and-the-democrats-won-so-big-trump-is-just-one-of-them/ |access-date=14 January 2026 |website=Cardinal News |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Education ===
Through her "Strengthening Virginia Schools Plan", Spanberger announced she would make higher education more affordable and accessible and make it easier for high school students to take college-level courses.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Spanberger supports reforming appointment process to reconcile differences at Va.'s colleges • Virginia Mercury |url=https://virginiamercury.com/briefs/spanberger-supports-reforming-appointment-process-to-reconcile-differences-at-va-s-colleges/ |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=Virginia Mercury |language=en-US}}</ref> She supports allowing teachers to deliver instruction and manage curriculum without interference from political bodies or agendas.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |last=Fuller |first=Bonnie |title=Abigail Spanberger: 'It's time to get culture wars out of our schools and let teachers teach' |url=https://vadogwood.com/2025/06/26/abigail-spanberger-its-time-to-get-culture-wars-out-of-our-schools-and-let-teachers-teach/ |access-date=2025-10-11 |website=vadogwood.com |language=en-US}}</ref> Spanberger has emphasized strengthening public schools by hiring and fairly compensating teachers, rather than supporting [[Private school#United States|private schools]] and [[School choice in the United States|school choice]].<ref name=":3" /> She also supports allowing schools to teach about Virginia's history of racism, saying, "history is important for us to learn from, for our kids to be proud of the progress we've made."<ref name=":3" />

===Environment===
[[File:Abigail Spanberger Tour 2024 - 54042915364.jpg|thumb|Director of Accelerator Operations Eduard Pozdeyev speaks with Congresswoman Abigail Spanberger, inside the CEBAF accelerator tunnel at [[Thomas Jefferson National Accelerator Facility|Jefferson Lab]] in [[Newport News, Virginia]] in September 2024.]]
Spanberger has called [[climate change]] "one of the greatest and most imminent threats to our economy, our national security, and our way of life" and said she will "stand up to attacks against science".<ref name="RTD10272018" /> During a 2019 Committee on Foreign Affairs meeting, she asked the Trump administration to reverse its isolationist policies, saying, "it's in our national interest to reinforce our stature as a global leader on international environmental and energy issues".<ref name="Envir">{{cite news|last=McCue|first=Dan|date=May 3, 2019|title=Citing Threats to National Security, Representative Elaine Luria, Va.-2, Takes Climate Change Head On|work=The WELL|url=https://www.thewellnews.com/citing-threats-to-national-security-representative-elaine-luria-va-2-takes-climate-change-head-on/|url-status=dead|access-date=April 13, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191217011452/https://www.thewellnews.com/citing-threats-to-national-security-representative-elaine-luria-va-2-takes-climate-change-head-on/|archive-date=December 17, 2019}}</ref>

Spanberger called the [[Green New Deal]] proposed by [[Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez]] a "bold compilation of ideas meant to address global climate change" but criticized it for allegedly including unrelated policy proposals and not identifying specific solutions to the problems it identifies, and said, "Overall, I am not a supporter of the Green New Deal".<ref name= "WaPo2202019">{{cite web |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/virginia-politics/rep-abigail-spanberger-quizzed-on-immigration-in-first-town-hall/2019/02/19/91305d08-3478-11e9-854a-7a14d7fec96a_story.html |title= Virginia Rep. Abigail Spanberger quizzed on immigration in first town hall |last= Portnoy |first= Jenna |date= February 20, 2019 |newspaper= The Washington Post |access-date= September 28, 2019}}</ref>

===Foreign affairs===
In February 2023, during the [[Russo-Ukrainian War|Russo–Ukrainian War]], Spanberger signed a letter advocating that President Biden give Ukraine [[General Dynamics F-16 Fighting Falcon variants|F-16]] fighter jets.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.politico.com/minutes/congress/02-21-2023/dems-join-f-16-call/ |title=Seven more lawmakers — including six Democrats — have signed on to a letter pushing Joe Biden to send F-16 jets to Ukraine. |date=February 21, 2023 |website=Politico |publisher= |access-date=February 24, 2023 |quote=}}</ref>

In June 2025, Spanberger supported the [[2025 U.S. strikes on Iranian nuclear sites|U.S. strikes on Iranian nuclear sites]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Virginia Gubernatorial Candidates Back Trump Strike On Iran |url=https://middlenecknews.com/local-news/virginia-gubernatorial-candidates-back-trump-strike-on-iran/ |access-date=2025-06-27 |website=MiddleNeckNews.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Gun control ===
Spanberger has called for a new version of the [[Federal Assault Weapons Ban]] that expired in 2004. She favors requiring [[background check]]s on private gun sales and supported a ban on [[bump stock]]s.<ref name="RTD10272018" /> Before she served in Congress, Spanberger volunteered with [[Moms Demand Action]], a gun-control advocacy group.<ref name=":4" />

===Health care===
Spanberger supports the [[Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act|Affordable Care Act]] (Obamacare).<ref name="RTD10272018" /> She supports a [[public option]] for healthcare via the proposed Medicare-X Choice Act.<ref name=":2" /> In November 2020, she called reducing the cost of prescription drugs "the top priority of families in my district".<ref name=":1" />

In January 2020, Spanberger sponsored the Public Disclosure of Drug Discounts Act, which passed the House unanimously. The bill requires [[Pharmacy benefit management|pharmacy benefit manager]]s (PBMs), who manage prescription drug benefits for health insurance companies, to publicize the rebates, discounts, and price concessions they negotiate, via a website hosted by the [[United States Secretary of Health and Human Services|U.S. secretary of health and human services]]. Spanberger also co-sponsored the [[Elijah Cummings Lower Drug Costs Now Act]], which grants Medicare Part D the power to negotiate prescription prices directly with drug companies.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Sullivan |first1=Heather |title=Spanberger bill aims to shed light on prescription drug pricing |url=https://www.nbc12.com/2020/01/17/spanberger-bill-aims-shed-light-prescription-drug-pricing/ |website=NBC12 |access-date=November 28, 2020 |date=January 17, 2020}}</ref>

===Immigration===
Spanberger objected to President Trump's [[Executive Order 13769|travel bans from certain predominantly Muslim countries]] and argued that they would aid jihadist propaganda by allowing a portrayal of the U.S. as an anti-Muslim country. She has voiced her support for stronger border security measures but opposes [[Trump wall|Trump's proposed wall]].<ref name="RTD10272018" /> Spanberger voted for a bill that included funding for border infrastructure, technology at ports of entry, and more customs and border protection officers and agents. She said she does not support "[[sanctuary city|sanctuary cities]]" but also called the term "a campaign slogan a lot of people get caught up in". She added that it "degrades the value of the conversation if we're not actually talking about what the real concern is."<ref name="WaPo2202019" /> Spanberger called for a pathway to legal status for illegal immigrants who abide by the laws, work, and pay taxes.<ref name="RTD10272018" />

Spanberger voted to allow [[U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement]] to be notified when undocumented immigrants attempt to purchase firearms.<ref name=":2" />

===LGBTQ rights===
Spanberger supports [[same-sex marriage]], saying in 2025, "All Virginians deserve the freedom to marry and for their families to be welcomed in our Commonwealth without the shadow of an outdated and unconstitutional ban on marriage equality lingering in Virginia's Constitution."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Graham |first1=Chris |title=Virginia Democrats pushing abortion, gay marriage, voting rights |url=https://augustafreepress.com/news/virginia-democrats-pushing-abortion-gay-marriage-voting-rights-measures/ |access-date=27 June 2025 |work=Augusta Free Press |date=15 February 2025}}</ref> In 2019, she voted in favor of the [[Equality Act (United States)|Equality Act]], which has not yet become law.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/house-bill/5|title=H.R.5 - 117th Congress (2021-2022): Equality Act|first=David N. [D-RI-1|last=Rep. Cicilline|date=March 17, 2021|website=Congress.gov}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://clerk.house.gov/Votes/2019217|title=Roll Call 217 Roll Call 217, Bill Number: H. R. 5, 116th Congress, 1st Session|date=May 17, 2019}}</ref> In 2022, she voted in favor of the [[Respect for Marriage Act]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Respect for Marriage Act of 2022 |url=https://ballotpedia.org/Respect_for_Marriage_Act_of_2022 |website=Ballotpedia}}</ref>

When Spanberger ran for governor in 2025, she was endorsed by the [[Human Rights Campaign]], an LGBTQ advocacy group.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Riley |first=John |date=2025-04-23 |title=Abigail Spanberger Backed by HRC in Virginia Governor Bid |url=https://www.metroweekly.com/2025/04/hrc-backs-abigail-spanberger-for-virginia-governor/ |access-date=2025-06-14 |website=Metro Weekly |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Personal life==
In April 2006 she married Adam Spanberger, a University of Virginia-trained engineer, and her high school sweetheart. They have three daughters together.<ref name=":4" /> She helped run a [[Girl Scouts of the USA|Girl Scouts]] troop for her daughters when they were young.<ref name=":4" />

In 2014, the family moved to [[Henrico County, Virginia|Henrico County]]. Before Spanberger became governor, they lived in [[Glen Allen, Virginia]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=King |first1=Sarah |title=Virginia's Future Is Female |url=https://richmondmagazine.com/news/field-notes/moms-rule/ |website=richmondmagazine.com |access-date=May 21, 2019 |language=en-us |date=May 12, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Walls |first=Ben |date=19 January 2026 |title=Spanberger family welcomes visitors to Governor's Mansion |url=https://www.wric.com/news/local-news/richmond/spanberger-family-executive-mansion-open-house/ |access-date=25 January 2026 |work=WRIC}}</ref>

Spanberger is a [[Protestantism in the United States|Protestant]].<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 3, 2019|title=Faith on the Hill: The religious composition of the 116th Congress|url=https://www.pewforum.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/7/2019/01/Detailed-tables-NUMBER-CHECK-COMPLETE-1-3.pdf|url-status=live|access-date=March 9, 2021|website=Pew Research Center's Religion & Public Life Project|language=en-US|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190105043212/http://www.pewforum.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/7/2019/01/Detailed-tables-NUMBER-CHECK-COMPLETE-1-3.pdf |archive-date=January 5, 2019 }}</ref>

During her time in Congress, Spanberger roomed with colleague [[Mikie Sherrill]], who was [[2025 New Jersey gubernatorial election|elected governor of New Jersey]] on the same day Spanberger was elected governor of Virginia.<ref name=":4" />

